\b\up0\fs28 Main form\tab\tab F2\tab\tab Show general data\par
\fs20\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\ri-432\qj\b0 In this form the general data, as listed in the 4nec2 input- or NEC output-file, is displayed. When the output-file is not yet generated, only few fields are filled.\par
\par
\pard\qj After the 4nec2 program is started, by default, the last loaded Nec2/4 file input-file is re-opened.\lang1043\f2\par
\lang1033\f0 This file represents the data you are working with. This data is used to generate near- of far-field patterns and may also used by the optimizer. \lang1043\f2\par
\pard\lang1033\f0 Using File -> {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "Open.htm"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2\ul open}}}\cf0\ulnone\v\f0\fs20 open\v0  you are able to choose and open another *.nec input-file. You are also allowed to select and open *.ant (antenna optimizer) or *.ez (Eznec) type input-files which are automatically converted to 4nec2 type input-files.\lang1043\f2\par
\lang1033\f0\~\lang1043\f2\par
\lang1033\f0 To support the starting antenna modeler, in version 5.3, a graphical based 3D \lquote Geometry editor\rquote  was included. The benefit of this editor is the fact that it is now much more easy to create/modify antenna structures. On the other side however, using this kind of editor, it is not possible to specify symbolic information as used by the optimizer/sweeper. For further information about using and selecting between the available types of editors, see \ul{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "IDH_F6.htm"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 Edit}}}\cf0\f0\fs20 .\ulnone\v IDH_F6\lang1043\v0\f2\par
\lang1033\f0\~\lang1043\f2\par
\lang1033\f0 It is furthermore possible to specify an output-file. If this is the case, the program displays the data present in the output-file, but it is not possible to generate new data or use the optimizer. This option is useful to compare intermediate output file data if {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "Save.htm"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2\ul saved}}}\cf0\ulnone\v\f0\fs20 save\v0  with a different name.\lang1043\f2\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\ri-432\qj\lang1033\f0\par
Please note that all numbers used in 4nec2 use the \b dot\b0  as the \b decimal seperator\b0 , independent of windows regional settings on the computer. So, especially for European users it is important to use the dot (\b .\b0 ) instead of the comma (\b ,\b0 ) when entering decimal numbers. \par
\par
Voltages and Currents specified and/or listed in the 4nec2 program use effective values. These are derived from the maximum values as listed in the NEC output-file by multiplication with 0.707. \par
\par
Furthermore the voltage- and current-values displayed and the field-strength values shown for the Near- and Far-field-pattern are in accordance with the input-power specified in the \lquote Settings\rquote  menu-bar option. For further info about this, see the \lquote Input-Power\rquote  paragraph in \uldb Settings\ulnone\v settings\v0 .\par
\par
With the menu-bar on this form you have access to the most important 4nec2 options and features. \par
\par
With the Calculate -> \uldb Generate NEC data\ulnone\v IDH_F7\v0  command it is possible to start the NEC-engine and generate new NEC output data. With the Calculate -> \uldb Optimize design\ulnone\v IDH_optim\v0  command you can start the 4nec2 optimizer to optimize data made available through the 4nec2 input file \par
Use Calculate -> \uldb Matching networks\ulnone\v IDH_F10\v0  to let 4nec2 automatically calculate L, Pi or T matching networks to create an 1:1 match and determine the losses for the selected network.\par
\par
With the \lquote Show -> voltage sources\rquote  or \lquote show-> RLC loading data\rquote  option it is possible to view detailed voltage source or RLC loading data, including SWR, delivered or dissipated power.\par
\par
Note however that if only two (connected) voltage sources are specified in the input file, it is assumed that a split voltage source is used. In the general data on the \lquote main (F2)\rquote  form and the impedance/SWR data on the \lquote line-chart (F5)\rquote  form the impedances and voltages are added. This is not the case of only one or more than two sources are specified.\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\ri-1800\qj\par
\pard\nowidctlpar Using \lquote File -> Import\rquote  one is able to import OpenPF or tab/white-space delimited far-field pattern data. This to let 4nec2 visualize the imported far-field pattern.\par
For a further description about the OpenPF format see \uldb Pattern\ulnone\v IDH_F4 IDH_F4\v0\par
\~\par
For an example of a tab/white space delimited far-field text file, open the file named \lquote FarFieldImportExample.txt\rquote  file in your \lquote ..\\4nec2\\plot folder\rquote .\par
Such a text file contains one or more sets of full/3D far field data. Each set is preceded by a line prefixed with a # and containing the text \lquote Frequency\rquote  with a numeric frequency value.\par
After this a number of Theta sets are listed (from 0 to 180 degrees), each containing the gain values for a full 2D horizontal/Phi pattern (0 to 360 degrees).\par
If (during import) an optional frequency, theta or phi angle is specified, the set for this specific entry is filtered out. Else the first/only set is loaded.\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\ri-1800\qj\par
\b Geometry builder\par
\b0\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\ri-432\qj Using the \lquote Run -> Geometry builder\rquote  menu-bar command one can start the \lquote Geometry builder\rquote . This is a powerfull tool to automatically create complex geometry structures using \lquote auto-segmentation\rquote  and/or \lquote equal-area rules\rquote . \par
To merge one or more structures to an existing model, open both the created *.nec and your 4nec2 file using Notepad and cut/paste the required cards/lines into your model. To remove overlapping wires, use \lquote Geometry-edit\rquote , select and remove wires one by one or select more wires by dragging a rectangle around them and remove them as a whole. \par
\pard\nowidctlpar\ri-1800\qj\page\super #$K\nosupersub\par
\b\fs28 Geometry form\tab\tab F3\tab\tab Show structure geometry\par
\b0\fs20\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\ri-432\qj In this form, the structure geometry (including voltage sources, transmission lines, (wire)loading and patches) as defined in the input file or as generated in the output file is displayed. With the cursor movement key's or mouse you can rotate and shift the structure. With the page-up/down buttons you can zoom-in/out on the structure. When currents display or far-field pattern display is on these buttons zoom in/out on currents or far-field pattern. Other options are available as both keyboard-buttons and menu-bar options.\par
\par
When no NEC output file is available the background is displayed in a none-white color, and some menu-bar options are disabled. When an output file is generated the data in this output-file is displayed. The background color is changed to white. The menu bar options are enabled according to the data in the output file.\par
\par
After editing the input file (by pressing F6), initially the (modified) input file data is displayed (none-white background). This is done so you directly can see the modifications you made to the input file. To see the resulting output file data you first have to generate a new output file by pressing the \uldb Generate\ulnone\v IDH_F7\v0  (F7) key.\par
\par
You can switch between I(nput) and O(utput) file view by pressing the \lquote I\rquote  or \lquote O\rquote  button when the Geometry form is on top.\par
\par
Wires/segments with a tag-number of 9800 or above are not displayed. This way it is possible to use far-away dummy wires while the main structure is still visible with normal scaling. Structure validation (see below) is also not performed on these dummy wires.\par
\par
You may left-click on any wire or segment to select a wire/segment and view additional information. To \lquote reset\rquote  click anywhere else on the form. If a wire/segment is selected and the right mouse button is pressed, the \lquote wire/segment\rquote  pop-up menu is displayed. One useful option in this menu is the \lquote center\rquote  option.  With this option you can set the rotation center for the structure on the middle of the selected wire/segment. With \lquote reset\rquote  you reset the center to the origin for the Y,Y,Z axis.\par
A selected wire is displayed in blue color with a filled and an open black circle. The closed circle is end-1, the open circle is end-2 for the selected wire.\par
\par
A further special and important option, which requires more explanation, is the \ldblquote Validate\rdblquote  menu-bar option. This option can be used to manually or automatic validate the most important NEC segment- and geometry-requirements. When the output-file view is active more segment checks are available than with input-file view. For a further description of what kind of checks are performed see \uldb Validate\ulnone\v validate\v0 .\par
\par
Automatic Geometry-checking is done before each Nec-run. Automatic Segment-checking is always done after the Nec-run. Because of the time-consuming nature of the Geometry-checks, one can enable or disable automatic geometry-checks, using the \ldblquote auto geo-check\rdblquote  menu option on the \ldblquote Geometry (F3)\rdblquote  form. Segment checking is always active.\par
\par
Further geometry validation can be done using \ldblquote Show -> Open ends\rdblquote . With this you you can check for open ends. Using \ldblquote Show->Junctions\rdblquote  you can check for the number of connected wires at junctions. \par
And last but not least you can use the \ldblquote\uldb 3D-viewer\ulnone\v IDH_F9\v0\rdblquote  to validate your structure. This viewer shows all wires with their real radius. This way it is very easy to see if certain wires intersect or not. At junctions with short fat segments you should check if a segments \lquote volume\rquote  does not lay more than about 1/3 in another segments \lquote volume\rquote .\par
\par
Under the \lquote Show\rquote  menu-bar option the following log-data is available:\par
\par
- Last NEC input\tab Pure Nec 2/4 input data which was send to the NEC engine for last run.\par
- Auto-segm log\tab\tab Logging about geometry modifications made by the auto-segmentation process\par
- Step-rad log\tab\tab Logging about geometry changes made by stepped radius correction process\par
- Symbol conversion\tab\tab Intermediate values for converted and calculated symbols/variables\tab\par
\par
The near- and far-field menu-bar options are only available if an output file is loaded which contains near- or far-field data, generated with the RP- or NE/NH card or by using the Generate (F7) command. You can toggle the display for the near/far-field on or off with the menu-bar option or with the \ldblquote R\rdblquote (adiation) button. \par
\par
When far-field display is enabled you can use the \lquote far-field\rquote  menu-bar option to let 4nec2 colorize the far-field pattern using gain/magnitude, tilt/phase, axial ratio or sense data from the output-file. \par
\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\ri-450\qj With the \ldblquote <\rdblquote  and \ldblquote >\rdblquote  key's it is possible to switch between the different hor/vert/total or X/Y/Z patterns.  If there are multiple patterns selected on the \ldblquote Pattern (F4)\rdblquote  form for comparison, these key's are used to switch directly between the far-field patterns for the different NEC output-files.\par
\par
Under the 'wire/segment' menu bar option you can 'walk' through al the structure wires/segments, select a specific wire/segment and display the wire geometry (input file) data or the segment current in polar or cartesian notation (output file). It is also possible to select a wire/segment by clicking with the mouse on or near a wire/segment. \par
\par
When the display of current-distibution is enabled, and also a freq-sweep calculation was performed, the function of the cursor/arrow key\rquote s is changed from structure movement/rotation to frequency \lquote movement\rquote . Using the left- and right arrow key\rquote s one can \lquote walk\rquote  through the frequencies included in the sweep and inspect the change in current-disribution over the structure while \~changing the frequency. \par
\~\par
Tip\i : for optimal display of current-distrubution with changing frequencies it\rquote s recommended to use current- instead of the default voltage-sources. See \uldb\i0 Edit.\ulnone\i\rquote  for more info about specifying excitation sources.\i0\par
\~\par
One can perform a frequency sweep by specifying a start-, stop- and step-frequency or by specifying a list of frequencies included in a text file. See \lquote\uldb Generate\rquote\ulnone\~ for more info about this subject.\par
\par
Use \lquote Currents->Multi color\rquote  and \lquote Currents->Incr./Dec. Log-factor\rquote  to visualize current distribution for small currents over gridded surfaces and/or large external structures.\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\ri-432\qj\par
You can use the \lquote Plot\rquote  menu-option to create and/or print linear 2D \uldb gnuplot\ulnone\v IDH_gpl\v0  graphs for one, more or all currents in the geometry structure.\par
\par
Use the \lquote File->Print\rquote  to capture and \uldb print/save\ulnone\v IDH_capt\v0  the geometry structure or other forms or windows.\par
\par
The 4nec2 tool was initially developed to assist on wire-geometry structures and the display of (arbritrary shaped) surface patches is not that sophisticated. For instance the current on patches are not visualized (yet), also structure validation is only performed in a limited way for patches.\par
Rectangular, triangular and quadrilateral patches are displayed according the specified XYZ- coordinates. For arbritrary shaped patches a square shape is assumed. However not always appropriate, this will give you well enough information about if center-, normal-vector and/or patch-area data are correctly specified.\par
\par
Under \lquote Show -> Povray\rquote  you may select between the generation of a true 3D geometry or far field pattern \uldb povray\ulnone\v povray\v0  file.\par
\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\ri-1800\qj\page\super\up10\fs13 #$K\nosupersub\up0\fs20\par
\b\fs24 Validate\tab\tab Validate geometry structure\par
\b0\fs20\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\ri-540\qj The Validate menu option on the Geometry (F3) form is used to check a large number of NEC requirements concerning the geometry structure and segmentation. With this, there are two different tests to distinguish:\par
\par
* Geometry checking/validation\par
* Segment checking/validation\par
\par
Because the \ldblquote Geometry check\rdblquote  (especially for models containing a large number of wires) can take some time, model validation is split into two different tests. Geometry validation is (optionally) performed before each Nec-run, Segment validation is automatically performed after each Nec-run. With \lquote set auto Geo-check\rdblquote  on the Geometry form you can enable/disable automatic geometry checking.\par
\par
After a geometry-check with errors and/or warnings a report-log is displayed. After a segment-check with errors a message is displayed. To get a report-log for the segment-check, manually run a segment-check.  After each test all wires/segments subjected to warnings or errors are displayed in grey and red colors. Use the left mouse-button, to select a wire and get specific segment error/warning info for the selected wire/segment.\par
\par
\par
\ul\b Geometry-check\ulnone\b0  validates the below:\ul\par
\ulnone\par
1)\tab Crossing or unconnected segments.\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\li380\ri-540\qj\par
This error condition occurs when the distance between two or more segments is below seg-length/1000 and the connection point is not a wire-end or a segment junction.\par
 \par
\pard\nowidctlpar\ri-540\qj 2)\tab Intersecting wires/segments.\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\li380\ri-540\qj\par
This warning is generated when the volumes of two or more wires are intersecting at a point other than their wire ends\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\fi380\ri-540\qj\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\ri-540\qj 3)\tab Sharp angles or too short/thick segments.\par
\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\fi4\li380\ri-540\qj This warning is generated when the intersecting volume of two or more wires, connected at their end-points or segment-junction becomes too large.\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\ri-540\qj\par
4)\tab Overlaid wires\par
\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\li380\ri-540\qj This error condition occurs it two or more wires are overlaid (same end-points)\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\ri-540\qj\par
5)\tab Unequal segmentation for parallel wires.\par
\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\li380\ri-540\qj This warning is generated if parallel wires within 1/20 wavelength distance and having an \lquote overlap\rquote  length of more than 1/10 wavelength have unequal segmentation.\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\ri-540\qj\par
6)\tab Vertical/horizontal wire height below xxx.\par
\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\li380\ri-540\qj This condition occurs when the height of one or both of the end-points for a certain wire goes below the limit as specified by its direction and the use of reflection coefficient or sommerfeld/norton ground.\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\ri-540\qj Note however that above warnings (or even errors) do not say that your model is not usable. They merely inform you about where extra attention might be needed to improve accuracy.\par
\par
\par
\ul\b Segment-check\ulnone\b0   validates the below:\ul\par
\ulnone\par
\cf1 (L = segment length, R = segment radius, wl = wavelength, A=area)\par
\cf0\par
Run segment-checks\tab\lquote 9\rquote\tab Generate error/warning report and view log-file\par
\par
Show all seg-checks\tab\lquote 0\rquote\tab Show results of all below checks.\par
\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\fi380\ri-540\qj\tab\tab\tab\tab\tab\tab\tab\tab\tab\tab\tab\tab\cf3 Warning(gray)\cf0\tab\cf4 Error (red)\cf0\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\ri-540\qj Segment Length\tab\tab\lquote 1\rquote\tab Check segment length\tab\tab\cf1 L  >  wl / 10\tab\tab L  >  wl / 5 or L < wl / 1000\cf0\par
Segment Radius\tab\tab\lquote 2\rquote\tab Check segment radius\tab\tab\cf1 R  >  wl / 100\tab R  >  wl / 30\cf0\par
Segm. Len / Rad\tab\tab\lquote 3\rquote\cf1  \tab Check length/radius \tab (def.)\tab L  <  8 * R\tab\tab L  <  2 * R\tab\cf0\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\fi380\li1520\ri-540\qj\cf1\tab\tab\tab\tab\tab\tab (EK)\tab L  <  2 * R\tab\tab L  <  0.5 * R\cf0  \par
\pard\nowidctlpar\ri-540\qj Junc. L big / L small\tab\lquote 4\rquote\tab Check length at junction\tab\tab\cf1 -\tab\tab\tab\tab L1 <  5 * L2\cf0\tab\tab\par
Junc. R big / R small\tab\lquote 5\rquote\tab Check radius at junction\tab\tab\cf1 R1 >  5 * R2\tab\tab R1 >  10 * R2\par
\cf0 Junc. Len / Rad\tab\tab\lquote 6\rquote\tab Check len/rad at junction\tab\tab L <\cf1   6 * R\tab\tab L  <  2 * R\cf0  \par
\par
Surf-patch area\tab\tab\lquote 7\rquote\tab Check max patch-area\tab\tab Error when :\tab A > (wl*wl)/25\par
\par
EX/LD requirem.\tab\lquote 8\rquote\tab Check EX/LD requirements\par
\par
\par
Furthermore the \uldb 3D-viewer\ulnone\v IDH_F9\v0  is also extremely usefull for further analyzing and detecting crossing, intersecting and/or overlaid (fat and short) wire-segments (at sharp junction angles).\par
\par
\f3 Additional validation for the geometry structure is possible by performing an \uldb Average-Gain\ulnone\v gain\v0  test or \uldb Convergence\ulnone\v Conv\v0  test\par
\par
\f0\page\super #$K\nosupersub\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\ri-1800\qj\b\fs28 Pattern form\tab\tab F4\tab\tab Show far- or near field pattern\par
\b0\fs20\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\ri-432\qj In this form the far- or near-field pattern data is displayed. \par
\par
\ul\b\fs24 Far-field display\par
\ulnone\b0\fs20\par
If far-field data is requested, the far-field is displayed in a polar diagram. You can select between linear- or the ARRL-style (semi-logarithmic) scaling. When scaling is in dB\rquote s, by default the ARRL-style is set to let the minor lobes be more distinguishable. When scaling is in V/m linear-scaling is set and recommended for use.\par
\par
With the arrow-key's you can 'walk' through the different Phi- or Theta-angles. When calculations for a range of frequencies are asked (frequency sweep), the left- and right-arrow keys are used to 'walk' through the frequency range.\par
If the \lquote display far-field option\rquote  is enabled on the \lquote Geometry (F3)\rquote  form, the selected pattern is highlighted in the complete 3D drawing. Selections made on the \lquote Pattern (F4)\rquote  form are reflected on the \lquote Geometry\rquote  form.\par
\par
When clicking on or near the far-field pattern line, the far-field data (angle and gain/field) for this point is displayed. Click anywhere else on the form to clear this data.\par
\par
With the 'Home' key you can select one of three normalization types (logged in the upper left) when viewing far-field Gain-data.\par
\par
Using the 'compare' option (\lquote Insert\rquote  and \lquote Remove\rquote  key\rquote s) you can compare far-field radiation patterns for two or more previously generated output-files. You may add a total of 5 different far-field patterns. You can also remove a pattern and add a different one. The only thing you must keep in mind is that the number of theta- and phi-steps for the patterns to compare must agree.\par
\par
With the '>' or \rquote ,\rquote  and '<' or \lquote .\rquote  keys you may cycle through the different far- or near-field columns/patterns as included in the output file data. The default pattern for the far-field is the 'Total gain' pattern. Using \lquote Far-field->Show Multi pattern\rquote  you can display all patterns together. \par
\par
To show the \ul circular polarized\ulnone  components RHCP- and, LHCP gain or E (left) and E (right) for the far-field pattern, check the \lquote show circular polarization\rquote  option on the \lquote Settings\rquote  menu and use the \lquote <\rquote  or \lquote >\rquote  keys to cycle between the circular  polarized components, or use the \lquote M\rquote  key to show all components using  a Multi-pattern view.\par
\par
With the 'G'(eometry) key or \lquote Show -> structure\rquote  option you can include structure geometry in the near- of far-field pattern display. Try the other menu-bar options to see what action they perform.\par
\par
\pard\nowidctlpar For information about beam-width, nr of lobes, max-gain and/or front to back ration you may enter the \lquote I\rquote (nfo) key or select \lquote show -< info\rquote . Also the \lquote J\rquote  key or \lquote show->indicator\rquote  may be usefull to get additional information. This indicator is also displayed if you click on or near the pattern-line.\par
When one or more patterns to compare are loaded and \lquote information\rquote  or \lquote indicator\rquote  is enabled,\~ the \lquote <\rquote  and \lquote >\rquote  keys are used to select between the different loaded patterns.\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\ri-432\qj\par
\pard\nowidctlpar Use the \lquote OpenPf\rquote  menu bar option to import or export far-field data using the OpenPF file format. This way one is able to exchange far-field data between 4nec2 and for instance Eznec or AO. This way could also store (export) generated far-field patterns for later usage, by importing it into another or similar model.\par
\~\par
\pard Using the \lquote FFtab\rquote  menu bar option one is able to generate an Eznec style FarField table. One (requested) usage for this could be to import the far field table into a program like Tant.exe for calculating Antenna Temperature and G/T ratio data. For further info see http://www.yu7ef.com/LowTemperatureAnt.htm\par
\~\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\ri-432\qj\par
You can use the \lquote Plot\rquote  menu-option to create and/or print none-polar 2D or 3D far-field graphs using the \uldb gnuplot\ulnone\v IDH_gpl\v0   program.\par
\par
\par
\ul\b\fs24 Near-field display\par
\ulnone\b0\fs20\par
If near-field data is requested, the near-field data is displayed in the XY, XZ or YZ plane. You can select a specific Z, Y or X position with the left of right arrow-keys. You can set the active display plane with the 'spacebar'. When pressing the Page-up and Page-down key's you can switch between this 2D view and a line-chart view in which the field-strength is displayed as function of the X, Y or Z position. In the 2D view you can select the vertical X, Y or Z position for the graph with the up- and down-arrow key's\par
\par
When displaying near-field data points on or in close proximity of the geometry structure this may result in a large maximum field strength value for certain field-points. This will also result in a large difference between the minimum and maximum field-strengths values, resulting in a low \lquote color-resolution\rquote  for the near-field display.  To avoid this, two options are available. First you can 'remove' field-points with field strength above a specified value. This is done with the 'Del' key in the pattern window. The second option is to set the linear field strength scale to a more (anti) logarithmic scale. This is done with the <Alt> and <page-up> or <page-down> keys. For both options also menu-items are included in the pattern form. These changes will also affect the display of the 3D pattern on the geometry form.\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\par
The default \lquote pattern\rquote  for the near field is the 'E/H Total' pattern. This 'E/H Total' pattern is not included in the output file data, but is computed as the 'sum' of the E/H(x), E/H(y) and E/H(z) fields. With the '>' or \rquote ,\rquote  and '<' or \lquote .\rquote  keys you may cycle through the different near-field patterns for E/H(x), E/H(y), E/H(z) and E/H(total).\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\ri-432\qj\par
\pard\nowidctlpar This \lquote total pattern\rquote  is calculated as below (as discussed by L.B.Cebik):\par
\~\par
Ex, Ey, Ez = magnitude given in peak volts/m or peak amps/m\par
Px,Py,Pz = phase angle of Ex, Ey, Ez\par
\~\par
Cp = Ex*Ex*cos(2*Px)+Ey*Ey*cos(2*Py)+Ez*Ez*cos(2*Pz)\par
Sp = Ex*Ex*sin(2*Px)+Ey*Ey*sin(2*Py)+Ez*Ez*sin(2*Pz)\par
Tp = Cp*Cp+Sp*Sp\par
\~\par
Etot = Sqrt ( 0.5 * (Ex*Ex+Ey*Ey+Ez*Ez+Sqrt(Tp) ) )\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\ri-432\qj\~\par
\pard\nowidctlpar Furthermore keep in mind that the data as shown in 4nec2 is presented in RMS volt/m or RMS amp/m and based on the power level set in the \lquote Settings\rquote  menu.\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\ri-432\qj\par
\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\ri-1800\qj\page\super #$K\nosupersub\par
\b\fs28 Line-chart form\tab\tab F5\tab\tab Show impedance, SWR and gain graphs\par
\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\ri-432\qj\b0\fs20 This form is used to display the input impedance, the SWR and, if specified, the gain, front-to-back (F/B) and front-to-rear (F/R) ratio as function of frequency- or variable-change on a line-chart. The form contains valid information if a frequency-sweep is requested with the \uldb Generate\ulnone\v IDH_F7\v0  (F7) command or when a \uldb Sweeping\ulnone\v Eval\v0 -action (F12) was performed. \par
\par
When the charts are too rude, try a smaller frequency/sweep step-size. If this is not possible you can smoothen the curve, by letting 4nec2 do a cubic-spline interpolation on the graph. Note however that this option can introduce considerable errors when the curve goes steep op or down, so increasing the number of calculation steps is recommended. Use \lquote View->Smooth\rquote  or the \ldblquote O\rdblquote  key to smooth the curve. \par
The maximum usable number of steps is set in the \lquote\uldb Settings\rquote\ulnone\v settings\v0  menu on the \lquote Main\rquote  form. \par
\par
Use \lquote Show -> Log X-scale\rquote  (\ldblquote F\rdblquote  key), to switch between linear and logarithmic frequency/variable axis scaling. Use \lquote Show -> Log Y-scale\rquote  (\ldblquote L\rdblquote  key) to switch between linear and logarithmic Y-axis scaling. Initially after a frequency- or variable-sweep the R-in, Z-in and SWR graphs are set to logarithmic scaling. The others default to linear scaling. If values pass the Y=0 line, like when X-in is changing from inductive to capacitive, the chart can not be set to logarithmic scaling.\par
\par
You may click anywhere on a graph to let 4nec2 display additional X,Y value information. Use the left/right arrow key to move the selection.\par
\par
The form can be resized by dragging the window-borders. \par
Try the menu-bar options to see what actions can be performed on the graphs.\par
\par
With the \lquote Tab\rquote  key you can select one or both of the Graph windows for changing the scaling or switching between Linear or Logarthmic (if possible).\par
\par
Impedance and SWR information is also available on the \uldb Smith-chart\ulnone\v IDH_F11\v0  (F11) form.\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\ri-1800\qj\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\ri-432\qj\b Note:\b0  If two (connected) voltage sources are specified in the input file, it is assumed that a split voltage source is used. In the general data on the \lquote main (F2)\rquote  form and the impedance/SWR data on the \lquote line-chart (F5)\rquote  form the impedances and voltages are added. This is not the case of only one or more than two sources are specified. \par
\par
If more than two voltage- or current sources are specified use \lquote V/I source -> previous/next\rquote  to switch the SWR- or Impedance-plot from one source to the other. \par
\pard\nowidctlpar\ri-1800\qj\par
\par
\par
\page\super #$K\nosupersub\par
\b\fs28 Edit\tab\tab F6\tab\tab Enter/modify or delete 4nec2 input data\par
\b0\fs20\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\ri-432\qj With this function you can view, enter or modify your 4nec2 antenna input-model. With version 5.3.0 and later can select between three different kind of entry systems, so called \lquote editors\rquote  to use: The Notepad-, \tab NEC-based- or the graphically-based geometry editor. You select between one of these editors with the \uldb Settings\ulnone\v settings\v0  menu-bar option. When pushing the F6 function key (or using Edit->Input file on the Main(F2) window) the selected editor is started and available for entering or changing your antenna-model/geometry-structure \par
With version 5.6.5 a fourth editor, based on the old NEC-editor was added. You can select between below four types of editor:\par
\par
\pard{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnf7\pnindent360{\pntxtb\'B7}}\nowidctlpar\fi-360\li360\ri-432\qj\tx360\uldb Notepad edit\ulnone\v Notepad\v0\par
\uldb{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}Nec-edit (old)\ulnone\v NecOld\v0\par
\uldb{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}Geometry-edit\ulnone\v GeoEdit\v0\par
\uldb{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}Nec-edit (new)\ulnone\v NecNew\v0\par
\pard{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlcont\pnf7\pnindent0{\pntxtb\'B7}}\nowidctlpar\ri-432\qj\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}The drag-and-draw style \lquote Geometry-editor\rquote  (or the new NEC-editor) is especially suited for the starting Antenna-Modeler, whereas the experienced NEC user may prefer \lquote Notepad-\rquote  or the old style NEC-editor. \par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
\ul{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
\pard{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlcont\pnf7\pnindent0{\pntxtb\'B7}}\nowidctlpar\ri-1800\qj\ulnone\super #$K\nosupersub\par
\pard{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlcont\pnf7\pnindent0{\pntxtb\'B7}}\nowidctlpar\ri-432\qj\ul\b\fs24 Notepad edit\b0\par
\fs20{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
\ulnone{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}This is the \lquote traditional\rquote  (4)nec2 windows-editor, which lets you directly modify the (4)nec2 input-file. When using this editor it is presumed the user has good knowledge about the NEC-syntax, because no syntax checking is done. On the other side this delivers maximum flexibility to use any kind of Nec-card and/or symbolic- and equational data.\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}One important difference between this Notepad- and the other editors is the fact that the Notepad-editor requires closing (or saving) the new data, before the NEC-engine can be run. The other editors can stay active when performing a NEC calculation (F7 key).\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}To create new 4nec2 input-files using Notepad, \uldb Open\ulnone\v open\v0  an existing NEC-file, edit it and save it using a different name. If done, use again the \lquote open\rquote  command to load the newly created file.\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
\pard{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlcont\pnf7\pnindent0{\pntxtb\'B7}}\nowidctlpar\ri-1800\qj\super #$K\nosupersub\par
\pard{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlcont\pnf7\pnindent0{\pntxtb\'B7}}\nowidctlpar\ri-432\qj\ul\b\fs24 Geometry edit\b0\par
\fs20{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
\ulnone{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}This drag-and-draw style geometry editor graphically supports the user in drawing/moving/rotating wires and placing sources, loads and transmission-lines. The user is guided in selecting between the available  source-, load- and ground-types. Pre-defined wire-radius, wire-conductivity and ground-parameter settings are available and one can select between metric, feet, inch or wavelength based length and/or radius units.\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}With this editor however it is not possible to specify symbolic (and equational) data to be used within the optimizer/sweeper. This due to the drag-and-draw based nature of the editor.\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}See \uldb Geometry-edit\ulnone\v Gedit\v0  below for further help-information.\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}See \uldb Menu-bar\ulnone\v Editmenu\v0  functions for a listing of available functions.\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
\i{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}n.b. If someone has suggestions for a general usable algorithm to convert 3D numerical wire end-coordinates to a SYmbol based lay-out, to be used by the optimizer/sweeper, please let me know. Despite numerous thoughts about this I did not yet succeed in finding an acceptable solution.\par
\i0{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
\cf2{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}However; in version 5.4.0, a first try was done to write the output of the Geometry editor to the corresponding *.nec file using symbols(variables) which then could be used by the optimizer to improve the model. Select \lquote Options -> Write symbols/variables\rquote  in the \lquote Geometry-edit\rquote  window to switch between default NEC syntax and 4nec2 symbolic syntax. Use \lquote distinguish XYZ coords\rquote  to avoid using the same symbol for both X,Y and/or Z coordinates when they are the same value.\par
\cf0{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
\ul{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
\pard{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlcont\pnf7\pnindent0{\pntxtb\'B7}}\nowidctlpar\ri-1800\qj\ulnone\super #$K\nosupersub\par
\pard{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlcont\pnf7\pnindent0{\pntxtb\'B7}}\nowidctlpar\ri-432\qj\ul\b\fs24 NEC editor (old)\b0\par
\fs20{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
\ulnone{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}This text-based NEC-syntax aware editor guides the user when entering or changing 4nec2 input-data. In contrast to the above editor, no pre-defined radius, conductivity or ground specifications are available, but it is throughout possible to include symbolic- and equational-data to be used within the optimizer or sweeper. \par
\pard{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlcont\pnf7\pnindent0{\pntxtb\'B7}}\nowidctlpar\li360\ri-432\qj\par
\pard{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlcont\pnf7\pnindent0{\pntxtb\'B7}}\nowidctlpar\ri-432\qj With this editor the selected GW, LD or EX card in the Edit window is highlighted on the Geometry window. If a wire is selected on the Geometry window, the corresponding GW line is highlighted in the Edit window. Any (wire)changes made in the Edit-window are reflected on the Geometry window whenever the current row on the Edit-window is changed\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}You may select one or more lines to be used for cut, copy and/or paste. Pressing SHIFT and clicking the mouse or pressing SHIFT and one of the arrow keys (UP ARROW, DOWN ARROW, LEFT ARROW, and RIGHT ARROW) extends the selection from the previously selected row item to the current row. Pressing CTRL and clicking the mouse selects or deselects a row in the list. You can also use copy/paste to copy and/or paste (using the clipboard) between the NEC-editor and other text-based NEC-(input)files.\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}To add a new NEC card, use \lquote new line\rquote  and select the required card-type from the \lquote card\rquote  combo-box. When selected use the <TAB> key to fill the next fields for the selected card-type. When field-entry is done, use up/down-arrow to add the new card-data to the list of NEC cards. As long as the card-data is not added to the list it is possible to change the card-type. Once added to the list it is not directly possible to change the card-type. To do so, first delete the current card-line/row.\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}While editing you do not have to save your data first to see the Nec results. Just click the \ldblquote Calculator\rdblquote  button or push <F7> to run the NEC-engine using the current data as displayed in the Edit window. Once you are satisfied (or not) you can save your data (or not) using \lquote File->Save(as)\rquote  \par
\ul{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
\pard{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlcont\pnf7\pnindent0{\pntxtb\'B7}}\nowidctlpar\ri-1800\qj\ulnone\super #$K\nosupersub\par
\pard{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlcont\pnf7\pnindent0{\pntxtb\'B7}}\nowidctlpar\ri-432\qj\ul\b\fs24 NEC editor (new)\b0\par
\fs20{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
\pard{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlcont\pnf7\pnindent0{\pntxtb\'B7}}\nowidctlpar\qj\ulnone Because I didn\rquote t like the old style Nec-editor, I decided to create a new spreadsheet style Nec-editor. For the moment the new Nec-editor is only Nec2 compatible, however in the near future Nec4 support will be added. For those using 4nec2 together with a Nec4 engine it\rquote s suggested to use the Notepad editor or the old style Nec-editor.\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}You can select the new style Nec-editor on the Main (F2) window by using \lquote settings->Nec edit (new)\rquote .\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}You can start the editor by using the F6 key (or select \lquote Edit -> Nec input-file\rquote ). After this a new window should show up containing multiple Tabs, some with an Excel style grid and some with ordinary text- and/or drop-down boxes. To navigate around between the individual cells in the Excel style grid, use the up/down/left/right arrow keys or directly select a cell using your mouse. When navigating between cells, the help-text box (just below the menu commands) is updated for each new cell.\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}This new Nec-edit window will show you below Tab pages:\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
\b{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}Symbols\par
\b0{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}On this page you can specify and/or modify all Symbols (variables) used in your model. At the bottom of this page you can select between different scaling methods. Scaling does affect the data on the Geometry page and all symbols used on this page. When \lquote custom\rquote  scaling is selected one can enter a user specific scaling factor. Note that you may also specify symbols to be used on the \lquote Source/load\rquote , \lquote Frequency/Ground\rquote  and other Tab-pages.\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
\cf4{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}Note: when using already existing Nec input (example) files it\rquote s possible that one or more SY variables have been re-used (defined more than once). These (position dependent) SY variables are mostly defined just before they are used in multiple GW, LD or EX lines. A warning message is generated when saving such a file.\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}To avoid unexpected results using the new Nec-editor, these files (which have been created with the Notepad- or old-style Nec-editor) need some slight modifications so that all used SY variables are unique throughout the Nec input-file.\par
\cf0{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
\b{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}Geometry\par
\b0{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}The Geometry page does contain all data used to specify the model\rquote s geometry structure. On the first column (named \lquote type\rquote ) you can select between the different geometry types:\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
\pard{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnf7\pnindent360{\pntxtb\'B7}}\nowidctlpar\fi-360\li360\qj\tx360 Wire, to specify a straight wire containing the specified number of segments\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}Helix, to specify a helical or spiral structure built from straight line segments\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}Arc, to specify a circular arc built from straight line wire segments\par
\pard{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlcont\pnf7\pnindent0{\pntxtb\'B7}}\nowidctlpar\qj\par
\pard{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnf7\pnindent360{\pntxtb\'B7}}\nowidctlpar\fi-360\li360\qj\tx360 copy/move, to move and/or copy one or more of the preceding geometry lines \par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}rotate, to rotate preceding geometry lines to create a cylindrical structure\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}mirror, to mirror the structure in X, Y and/or Z-plane to benefit from symmetry\par
\pard{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlcont\pnf7\pnindent0{\pntxtb\'B7}}\nowidctlpar\qj\par
\pard{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnf7\pnindent360{\pntxtb\'B7}}\nowidctlpar\fi-360\li360\qj\tx360 arbitrary shaped surface-patch, to specify an arbitrary surface with known area \par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}triangular sp, ditto for a triangular shape\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}rectangular sp, ditto for a rectangular shape\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}quadrilateral sp, ditto for a quadrilateral shape\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}patch continuation, to specify a string of rectangular/quadrilateral patches\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}multiple (rectangular) sp, to cover a rectangular region with a number of rectangular patches\par
\pard{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlcont\pnf7\pnindent0{\pntxtb\'B7}}\nowidctlpar\qj\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}To specify tapered wires, both in segment-length and/or in wire-radius, check the \lquote use wire tapering\rquote  box. If checked, additional columns for straight wire rows will show up.\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
\b{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}Source/Load\par
\b0{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}Using the check-boxes at the top of this page, one can specify additional antenna structure data such as:\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
\pard{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnf7\pnindent360{\pntxtb\'B7}}\nowidctlpar\fi-360\li360\qj\tx360 Excitation sources; at least one source, mostly a voltage- or current-source, is needed to get useful output-data\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}Wire/RLC loads; used to specify wire conductivity, trap/RLC loads or wire insulation. You may use a single or multiple antenna materials.\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}Transmission lines; to model open-wire or coaxial feed/transmission lines. These are equational lines and do not contribute to the radiation pattern.\par
\pard{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlcont\pnf7\pnindent0{\pntxtb\'B7}}\nowidctlpar\qj\par
\b{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}Frequency/Ground\par
\b0{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}With this page you can specify the environment in which the antenna is placed. A (design) frequency specification is required. When no ground is specified, free space is used. \par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}If the antenna is placed above none-perfect ground, one can use the drop-down boxes to select between different ground types. Select \lquote user-specified\rquote  to manually enter specific ground conditions. \par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}Check \lquote use ground screen\rquote  to model an additional radial wire ground screen.\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}Check \lquote use second ground\rquote  to model a two-stage ground as on a hill-top or near a cliff or (salty) beach.\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab} \par
\b{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}Others\par
\b0{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}This page is used to enter more specific Nec2 data. You may (un)check \lquote Ground connection\rquote  if you would like to have your wires with an end at Z=0 to be connected or isolated from ground. \par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}The \lquote Read/Write geometry\rquote  box is used to read or write predefined geometry structures. \par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}There\rquote s no harm in always using the \lquote extended kernel\rquote\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
\b{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}Comment\par
\b0{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}To provide your model with additional comments, use this page. \par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
\ul{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
\b{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}Menu-bar commands\par
\ulnone\b0{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}On top of the Nec-edit window a number of menu-bar commands are available. Most of them will be familiar to the average computer user, however some require additional explanation. Under the \lquote Cell\rquote  menu-option, cut/copy/paste commands are available for editing a single cell. Under the \lquote Rows\rquote  menu-option, cut/copy/paste commands are available for editing multiple rows at once.\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}Under the \lquote Selection\rquote  menu-option commands are available to perform certain actions on a number of selected cells. To select one or more cells, hold down left mouse-button and drag around over the cells you would like to include in the selection. Or click on the upmost row  or leftmost column to select a specific column or row. After a selection has been made you can use \lquote make same value\rquote , \lquote add same value\rquote  or \lquote multiply same value\rquote  to treat all selected cells the same way. Use \lquote make same value\rquote  to enter equal values in all selected cells. Use \lquote add/multiply same value\rquote  to add or multiply existing cell values with a second user supplied value.\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}Use \lquote Options->Auto update geometry window\rquote  to let 4nec2 automatically update the Geometry (F3) window after each change. However if a complex structure is specified using large number of geometry lines this updating could become irritatingly slow. If this is the fact, uncheck the menu-bar option.\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}To let 4nec2 automatically resize column-widths when window size is changed, check the \lquote autoresize columns\rquote  option.\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}You can individually change column widths by clicking and dragging the column separator bars on the upmost row (containing the header names). These settings will be saved, so next time when Nec-edit is started, your column widths will be restored.   \par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
\ul\b{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}Command buttons\par
\b0{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
\ulnone{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}Two command buttons have been placed on the Edit window. From right to left, a Save button and a Calculate button. The Save button saves all changes to the current file (as does File->Save). The Calculate button does start a new Nec calculation using actual data, whether or not this data is saved before. If you don\rquote t like the outcome resulting from latest changes you can quit the edit window without saving.\par
\f3{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
\pard{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlcont\pnf7\pnindent0{\pntxtb\'B7}}\nowidctlpar\ri-1800\qj\f0\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
\super{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}#$K\nosupersub\par
\pard{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlcont\pnf7\pnindent0{\pntxtb\'B7}}\nowidctlpar\ri-432\qj\ul\b\fs24 Geometry Edit\b0\par
\ulnone\fs20{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}This editor is used to specify the antenna structure like creating a (schematic) drawing. In the edit window a 2D or 3D-view of the antenna input-file geometry is displayed which can be modified using select-, draw- and drag/drop methods. \par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}To switch between 2D and 3D view, select one of the four leftmost view-mode buttons labeled \ldblquote 3D\rdblquote , \ldblquote XZ\rdblquote ,\rdblquote YZ\rdblquote  or \ldblquote XY\rdblquote  on the upper left of the Edit-window. For 2D view you can switch between XZ, YZ or XY plane. \par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
