}\f0{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\tab\tab Add, modify or remove ground specifications.\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}When viewing a structure or modifying none-wire objects you may use either 3D or 2D view. When modifying Wire-geometry it is advised to use only the 2D-view to avoid unexpected results. This because when moving a wire in 3D-view it automatically connects to the nearest available wire. To undo a faulty movement, use the right mouse-button while the mouse-pointer is over the selected wire or use the \lquote edit\rquote  menu.\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}To select between metric, feet, inch or wavelength as the  length/radius units use the \lquote Settings\rquote  menu-bar option in the \ldblquote Main\rdblquote  window.\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
\ul{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}Modify existing objects\par
\ulnone{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}To modify an existing object, select the type of object (wire, source, load, etc...) by clicking the corresponding top-row object-button. If a none drag/drop-type object is selected the corresponding data is directly available on the right part of the edit-window. \par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}When a \lquote drag/drop\rquote  type object is selected you will also have to select the desired object from the range of similar objects by clicking on the appropriate item in the 2D or 3D picture or by using the scroll-bar on the mid-right part of the screen.\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}If an object is selected the wire or segment on which it is located is highlighted and the mouse-pointer changes from the default arrow to a two- or four-point arrow, indicating that the object is ready for moving. The corresponding object data is available for textual editing on the right of the screen. If a two-point arrow is visible, the whole wire is movable. When a four-point arrow is visible only one end is moved.\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
\ul{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}Note:\ulnone  When modifying a drop-down-list or a text-box-value, enter the <Tab> key, click another text-box or anywhere inside the picture-box to let 4nec2 know that the value has changed and the model can be repainted. \par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}In 2D-view, wires can be moved to any other 2D position. According to the \ldblquote Snap to grid\rdblquote  or \ldblquote Snap to wire\rdblquote  check box setting, the moved wire-end(s) \lquote snap\rquote  to the nearest grid-position or to another near wire-end. When a wire is moved in 3D-view it always automatically connects to the nearest wire. The \lquote snap\rquote -range for \lquote Snap-to-wire\rquote  corresponds to roughly half the grid-line spacing.\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab} \par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}When a wire is selected the wire is displayed with a green and red open or filled box on either side of the wire. The bleu box represents end-1, the red box end-2. A filled (blue or red) box indicates that this wire-end is connected to one or more other wires. (positive NEC current flows from end-1 to end-2, so in the direction of end-2. Think about the \lquote red\rquote  end-1 as the back-light of a car).\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}To release connected wires or to keep them connected while moving, (un)check the \ldblquote Keep connected\rdblquote  check box within the wire-data. This option is also in effect when modifying the XYZ end-position, length or theta/phi wire-data text boxes. \par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}When a two- or four-point mouse-pointer is visible one can use the \ul right\ulnone  mouse-button to perform additional edit operations. It is possible to connect or disconnect a certain wire-end by using the right-mouse button while the mouse-pointer is over a selected wire-end. \ldblquote Disconnect end\rdblquote  or \ldblquote Connect to near wire\rdblquote  are only available when the mouse-pointer is over a certain wire-end. Most other actions are available if a certain wire is selected.\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}When the mouse-pointer is not located over an object (default pointer visible) the right mouse-button is used to shift the whole picture up/down or left/right.\par
\ul{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
\ulnone{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}Sources, Loads and Tr-line(ends) can only be moved from one wire/segment to another wire/segment. They automatically snap to a near segment. The \lquote snap-range\rquote  for this equals half the grid-line spacing.\par
\ul{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}Add new objects\par
\ulnone{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}To add new objects, select the \ldblquote Add\rdblquote  button. The mouse-pointer changes to a cross-hair indicating \ldblquote Add\rdblquote  mode is active.\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}When object-type \ldblquote Wire\rdblquote  is selected (2D view), click inside the picture-box to set end-1, hold down the button, draw the wire and release to set end-2. By default the third (depth)dimension in the 2D view is set to zero (highlighted text-box on right side of screen). You may modify this value to create objects for other \lquote depth\rquote -coordinates. \par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}Use \ldblquote Options->Hide wires not in plane\rdblquote  to draw overlapping or almost overlapping 2D- structures for different \lquote depth\rquote -coordinates  (for instance cubical quads). This will avoid selecting the wrong wire and new or moved wires do not accidentally connect to wires lying in another \lquote depth\rquote  plane.\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}When a wire is created or the length for a wire is changed the number of segments is automatically updated according the specified \ldblquote Set segmentation\rdblquote  option. You can select between \ldblquote low\rdblquote , \ldblquote medium\rdblquote  and \ldblquote high\rdblquote  segment-count in the \ldblquote Options\rdblquote  menu-bar option, corresponding to 10, 25 or 100 segments per half/wave. When a whole wire is moved the number of segments is not changed.  \par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}To avoid updating the segmentation when wire length is changed, select/check \ldblquote Stop segmentation\rdblquote  in the above menu. This could be (temporary) usefull when a precise segment-count was already specified due to short wires/sharp bents\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}If the (design)frequency for the model is changed the segment-counts are not automatically updated. Use the \ldblquote Recalculate segm.\rdblquote  option to let 4nec2 recalculate the segmentation for the selected wire(s) according the specified segmentation density and new frequency. (use Ctrl+A to select all wires).  \par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}When creating new wires, it is not strictly needed to position the mouse-pointer on the exact XYZ coordinates. After having drawn (part of) the required structure you may select a wire and use the end-1 or -2 edit-boxes on the right part of the window to specify more exact coordinates. Use the end-1 or -2 option-boxes to specify which wire-end should stay in position. Use the \lquote Keep connected\rquote  box to specify whether other connected wires should stay connected or not after modifying the wire-data. \par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}The default radius used for new wires is made equal to the radius specified for the last added/create wire (available wire with the highest wire-number). When adding the first wire to a new model, the wire-radius is explicitely asked for. Use \ldblquote Snap to grid\rdblquote  to place wire-end coordinates relative to the specified grid. Use \lquote Snap to wire\rquote  to automatically connect a moved wire-end to another nearby wire-end.\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab} \par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}To add a source- or load-object, click the desired object-type button, click anywhere inside the picture-box to create a new source/load object and drag the source/load onto the desired wire/segment using the left mouse-button. When done, edit the source/load data on the right part of the screen to select between different source/load types and to specify additional source/load data. \par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}To add a Transmission-line object, click the Tr-line button and click on the desired wire/segment to set end-1 of the new Transmission-line. Hold down the left mouse-button and move onto the second wire/segment to set the other end-2 of the new Transmission-line. Edit the data on the right part of the screen to set char-impedance, line length and/or crossed line.\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}To create/modify ground parameters use the right-most button and select between free-space or the other available ground-types. To specify a linear or radial cliff, select the \ldblquote second/far ground\rdblquote  box to specify additional ground data.  \par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}Use the \ldblquote Create\rdblquote  menu to create rectangular-, grid-style-, circular- or helix-type shapes. Using the \lquote circle\rquote  option you can also create triangles, squares and other n-sided structures by specifying a number of sections of 3 or 4 or more. You can create wire-arcs by deleting one or more wires from a previous created circle. \par
\ul{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}Selecting multiple wires\par
\ulnone{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}To select multiple wires for moving, deleting, copying or pasting click anywhere near a wire, hold down the left mouse-button and move the \lquote selection-box\rquote  over the wires to select. When 3D view is active hold down the <Shift> key to avoid rotating the 3D structure. To add distinct wires to the current selection, use the <Ctrl> key while selecting other wire(s). When in 2D view it is sometimes possible not all selected wires are visualized (red color), because one or more wires may overlap each other.\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}A selection of multiple wire always contains a \lquote main\rquote  wire. This wire is displayed as a more thick line with a green and red open/filled box on either side. To move a selection of multiple wires, locate the mousepointer over this \lquote main\rquote  wire till the mouse-pointer changes to a 2-point arrow. When no previous wire was selected the \lquote main\rquote  wire is the first of all selected wires. If a single wire was previously selected the selected wire becomes the \lquote main\rquote  wire.\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}When moving multiple wires the complete selection is snapped to a grid-point or nearest wire indicated by the end-1 or end-2 of this \lquote main\rquote  wire. To \lquote snap\rquote  for a certain end, click near the required end when moving is started.\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}When having selected one or more wires you can use the right mouse-button to perform additional actions. \par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}One special operation is the \ldblquote Rotate and Move\rdblquote  action. With this operation (similar to the original NEC based GM card) you can rotate and/or move selected wires or you can create one or more similar structures, for instance to create a stack of yagi\rquote s. When using the rotate and move operation, it is important to keep in mind the order of transformations. First, all selected wires are rotated around the X-axis for the specified amount of clockwise degrees followed by rotation around the Y-axis, followed by rotation around the X-axis. Finally the resulting structure is moved according the specified X,Y,Z distances.\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}When the \lquote Create N new structures\rquote  option is selected, each new structure is created with the selected or previous created structure as the reference for the next transformation.\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}Another operation is \ldblquote Rescale wire(s)\rdblquote . This operation is used to scale all selected wires to a new frequency or by a specified scaling-factor. This scaling involves both wire length/coordinates and wire-radius.\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}Two \b important\b0  things to keep in mind using above operations is the fact that the \ldblquote Keep connected\rdblquote  check-box is not in use (unselected but connected wires are not moved) and these operations can only be undone by performing \lquote reverse\rquote  operations.\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}To rotate a certain wire around one of his ends, first move the end to the center of the XYZ-axis, do the rotation and then move the wire back, or directly edit the the Phi/Azim or Theta/Elev text boxes on the right part of the screen. If the latter action is performed, bu sure you selected the correct \ldblquote Fix end-1\rdblquote  or \ldblquote end-2\rdblquote  check-box.\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}You can use the \ldblquote Resequence trag-nr\rdblquote  operation to renumber the tag-nr\rquote s after creating, cutting, copying and/or pasting one or more wires.\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
\i{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}Tip\i0 :\tab Use the 3D-viewer together with the Geometry-edit function and meanwhile switch between both windows to see the immediate 3D results when changing wire lengths, posistions and/or radius. This to avoid overlaid or intersecting wires.\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
\ul{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}Spread-sheet wire-table edit\par
\ulnone{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}Another way to modify Wire-data is by selecting the \ldblquote Wire data/Comment\rdblquote  object-button. All wire data is displayed in a spreadsheet like grid for textual editing. You can select, remove, delete cut and/or copy one or more wires to get the wire sequence that suits your needs. You can also cut and/or paste textual (wire) content to or from another document.\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}You can use the operations in the \ldblquote Edit\rdblquote  menu to perform similar actions on one or more selected cells, rows or columns. For instance to change wire-diameter for the whole or part of the structure or set the segment-count to user required values.\par
\ul{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}Other\par
\ulnone{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}Select the \ldblquote Show Nec\rdblquote  menu-bar option to see the corresponding NEC cards and to become used to the NEC syntax. Use \ldblquote Options -> Write symbols/variables\rdblquote  to switch between default NEC and symbolic 4nec2 syntax.\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}To create a new design, use \ldblquote New\rdblquote  from the \ldblquote File\rdblquote  menu. This will clear the drawing and reset all data. Before starting the drawing it is required to enter the \lquote design frequency\rquote . This will also initialize the grid/axis-size according the specified wavelength. \par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}While editing you do not have to save your data first to see the Nec results. Just click the \ldblquote Calculator\rdblquote  button or push <F7> to run the NEC-engine using the current data. Once you are satisfied (or not) you can save your data (or not) using \lquote File->Save(as)\rquote  \par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}Some shortcut-key\rquote s:\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}W\tab show/hide wire-nr\tab\tab\tab X\tab set YZ plane\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}T\tab show/hide tag-nr\tab\tab\tab\tab Y\tab set XZ plane\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}E\tab show/hide ends\tab\tab\tab\tab Z\tab set XY plane\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}S\tab show/hide segments\tab\tab\tab 3\tab set 3D view\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}<Home>\tab\tab\tab\tab\tab\tab Reset view\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}<Page-up>/Page-down>\tab\tab\tab Structure zoom in/out\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}Shift + <Page-up>/Page-down>\tab Grid zoom in/out\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}<up>/<down>/<left>/<right>\tab\tab Rotate 3D view\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
