\ul\fs24{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}Notepad- and Nec-edit\par
\ulnone\b0\fs20{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}The default/minimal lay-out for a simple 4nec2 input file is the following:\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}Zero or more CM (comment) cards.\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}One CE (comment end) card\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}One or more GW (wire geometry) cards\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}One GE (geometry end) card\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}One FR (design frequency) card\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}One or more EX (excitation point) cards\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}Zero or one GN (Ground condition) card\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}Zero or more LD (loading) cards\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}One EN (end of file) card\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}You may include comment in the input file used by 4nec2 by using the  ' character (single quotation mark) anywhere in the input file. See also the example files on your 4nec2 home-folder.\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}You may also hide specific (far away) wires in your geometry structure by using tag-numbers in the range 9800-9900. This way the model initially might be more correctly scaled. Don\rquote t specify tag-numbers above 9900, because these tags are internally used by 4nec2 to model Current-sources.\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}To use the built-in 4nec2 Current-source Excitation-type see \ul Current-sources (EX 6)\ulnone\v I-src\v0\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}To use the built-in 4nec2 LC-trap Loading-or Wire-coat loading type see \uldb LC-Traps (LD 6)\ulnone\v Trap\v0  and \uldb Wire coat (LD 7)\ulnone\v Coat\v0\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}For additional ground- and/or wire-loading data see \uldb Ground conditions\ulnone\v ground\v0  or \uldb (wire) loading\ulnone\v loading\v0\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}All text after the first EN card is ignored, so you may use this area for temporary cutting and pasting of NEC cards.\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}With version 5.7.0 and later it\rquote s also possible to enter the position of Voltage/Current sources (EX), Spot-loads (LD) and Transmission lines/Networks (TL/NT) on the wire in percents from the beginning (end-1). \par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}You do this by entering a value from 0 to 100 followed by a percent (%) sign. 4nec2 will automatically convert this percentile distance to the required segment-number. Note however that the more segments a wire consists of the more precise the positioning will be related to the specified percentile value. \par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}For those who are not yet familiar with the NEC syntax and/or antenna modeling, there are a lot of recommendable articles, written by L.B. Cebik (W4RNL), available on the internet.\par
\up10\fs13{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
\pard{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlcont\pnf7\pnindent0{\pntxtb\'B7}}\nowidctlpar\qj\super\up0\fs20 #$K\nosupersub\par
\pard{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlcont\pnf7\pnindent0{\pntxtb\'B7}}\nowidctlpar\ri-1800\qj\b\fs24 EX 6\tab Specifying Current Sources\par
\pard{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlcont\pnf7\pnindent0{\pntxtb\'B7}}\nowidctlpar\qj\b0\fs20\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}Besides the use of the standard NEC Voltage Sources (EX 1), 4nec2 also supports the use of Current Sources. To make this work, the standard NEC type for the EX card is extended with an additional type 6 specifying a current source. When using a text editor as the 4nec2 entry system the fields for this card are the same as those for the voltage source, however the real and imaginary parts are specified in Amperes \par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}When using the \lquote geometry editor\rquote , current-sources are automatically created when \lquote Current\rquote  is selected as the excitation source type.\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}Internally in 4nec2 this EX type 6 is converted to a voltage-source on a dummy segment and connected to the initial segment through a network NT-card. To see the results of the internal current- to voltage source conversion, use \lquote Show -> Nec 2 input\rquote  on the \lquote Geometry (F3)\rquote  window after a (4)Nec2 calculation is done or select the \lquote show Nec\rquote  menubar option when using the \lquote geometry editor\rquote .\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}Mostly the default Nec voltage-source (EX 0) is used. When modeling with multiple sources where the current for each source requires a specific phase relationship, the use of current-sources (EX 6) is needed. \par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
\b{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}Split sources\par
\b0{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}When two sources are specified on two nearby and connected segments, a so called split-source is assumed. Such a split-source is usefull to specify a source on a segment junction, such as could be used for an inverted-V antenna. Furthermore split-sources are very usefull to preserve symmetry when a GX card is involved. This to minimize calculation time.\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}For a split-source impedances and voltages are added on the \lquote Main (F2)\rquote  and \lquote Line-chart (F5)\rquote  windows. This to represent a single source.\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
\super{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}#$K\nosupersub\par
\pard{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlcont\pnf7\pnindent0{\pntxtb\'B7}}\nowidctlpar\ri-1800\qj\b\fs24 LD 6\tab Specifying LC-Traps\par
\pard{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlcont\pnf7\pnindent0{\pntxtb\'B7}}\nowidctlpar\qj\b0\fs20\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}Together with the use of the standard NEC Loading types from LD1 to LD5, 4nec2 also supports the use of the LC-Trap loading type. To make this work, the standard NEC type for the LD card is extended with an additional type 6; specifying an LC-trap. The fields for this card are the same as for the parallel/Series RLC card, except for the R(esistance) field. For the LC-trap this field (F1) represents the unloaded\endash Q of the inductor. A zero in this field defaults to a unloaded-Q of 100.\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}When using the \lquote Geometry\rquote  editor you can select \lquote LC-trap\rquote  after adding a new or selecting an existing RLC-load object. The corresponding LD card is automatically created.\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}When using a text editor as the 4nec2 entry system the LD 6 card has the below syntax:\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
\b{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}Cmd\tab Tag-nr\tab\tab Start-seg\tab End-seg\tab coil-Q\tab\tab L (in H)\tab\tab C (in F)\tab\tab [fr]\par
\b0{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}LD\tab 6\tab 2\tab\tab\tab 1\tab\tab\tab 16\tab\tab\tab 16\tab\tab\tab 5.5e-6\tab\tab 45e-12\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}Internally in 4nec2 this LD-type 6 is converted to a parallel-RLC loading type. For calculating the coil loss-resistance (which depends both on coil-Q and the reactance X of the coil) the design frequency from the nec input-file (First FR card) is used to get to coil reactance. \par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}Optionally you can specify different frequencies to use for reactance- and thus loss-calculation for each coil/trap.\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}Internally in 4nec2 this LD-type 6 is converted to parallel-RLC loading type using the frequency value as specified on the initial FR-card. To see the results of the internal LD conversion, use \lquote Show -> Nec 2 input\rquote  on the \lquote Geometry (F3)\rquote  window after a (4)Nec2 calculation is done or select the \lquote Show Nec\rquote  menubar option when using the \lquote geometry editor\rquote .\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}You may also specify the start- en end-segments as a percentile distance (0 to 100) from the beginning of the wire.\par
\super{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}#$K\nosupersub\par
\pard{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlcont\pnf7\pnindent0{\pntxtb\'B7}}\nowidctlpar\ri-1800\qj\b\fs24 LD 7\tab Specifying Insulated wires (Wire-coating)\par
\pard{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlcont\pnf7\pnindent0{\pntxtb\'B7}}\nowidctlpar\qj\b0\fs20\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}Besides the default NEC Loading types from LD1 to LD5, 4nec2 also enables you to specify data for the insulation wrapped around conducting wires. This insulation produces shortening effects in the specified wires, thus lowering the velocity factor. To make this work, 4nec2 uses an extra LD7 type.\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}The method used in this was described by L.B. Cebik in \lquote www.cebik.com/amod/amod83.htm\rquote l\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}When specifying insulated wires, there are two radii of interest. First the radius of the conducting wire (as specified in the GW card) and second the radius of the wire plus insulation. The difference between both values is the thickness of the insulation layer. \par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}Furthermore the dielectric constant for the insulation material is of interest. In 4nec2 a high insulating effect (very low conductivity) for the material is presumed.\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}On the internet the below \b dielectric constants\b0  were found:\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}Aluminium-oxide\tab\tab 10\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}Bakelite\tab\tab\tab\tab 3.5 - 4.5\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}Copper-oxide\tab\tab 18.1\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}Glass\tab\tab\tab\tab 5.0 - 9.0\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}Glass (window)\tab\tab 7.6\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}Mica\tab\tab\tab\tab 4.0 - 8.0\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}Neoprene\tab\tab\tab 4.0 - 6.7\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}Oil\tab\tab\tab\tab\tab 1.5 - 4.7\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}Paper\tab\tab\tab\tab 1.6 - 2.6\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}Parrafin\tab\tab\tab\tab 2.0 - 3.0\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}Pertinax\tab\tab\tab\tab 4.3 - 5.5\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}Plexiglas\tab\tab\tab 2.6 - 3.5\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}Polycarbonate\tab\tab 2.9 - 3.2\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}Polyethylene\tab\tab 2.4\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}Polyamide (nylon) \tab 3.4 - 3.5\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}Polystyrene\tab\tab\tab 2.4 - 3.0\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}Porcelain\tab\tab\tab 5.0 - 6.5\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}PVC (hard)\tab\tab\tab 3.0 - 4.0\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}PVC (soft)\tab\tab\tab 4.0 - 5.0\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}Rubber\tab\tab\tab\tab 2.7 - 3.2\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}Shellac (Nat.)\tab\tab 2.9 - 3.9\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}Styrofoam\tab\tab\tab 1.03\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}Teflon\tab\tab\tab\tab 2.1\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}Water (destil)\tab\tab 34 - 78\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}Wood (dry)\tab\tab\tab 1.4 - 2.9\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\tab\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}Most plastics appear to have a dielectric constant (permittivity) between 2.0 and 3.5. The dielectric constant of air is around 1.0, so if we would specify a value of 1.0, no matter what radius we would get the performance of bare wire.\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}When using the \lquote Geometry\rquote  editor you can select \lquote Coat\rquote  after adding a new or selecting an existing RLC-load object. The corresponding LD card is automatically created.\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}When using a text editor as the 4nec2 entry system the LD 7 card has the below syntax:\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
\b{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}Cmd\tab Tag-nr\tab\tab Start-seg\tab End-seg\tab Diel-const\tab Coat-radius (mtr)\par
\b0{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}LD\tab 7\tab 2\tab\tab\tab 1\tab\tab\tab 16\tab\tab\tab 2.8\tab\tab\tab .003\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}The parameters \lquote Tag=nr\rquote , \lquote Start-seg\rquote  and \lquote End-seg\rquote  are the same as the corresponding parameters used by the LD 0 through LD 5 command\rquote s.\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}You may also specify these start- en end-segments as a percentile distance (0 to 100) from the beginning of the wire.\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}The first floating point parameter \lquote Diel-Const\rquote , specifies the dielectric constant for the insulation material used. The second floating-point parameter specifies the overall radius for the wire plus insulation. \par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}Internally the LD7 command is converted to an LD2 command using the below equation:\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
\b{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\tab\tab\lang1031 L = 2e-7 * (diel * R/r) ^ (1/12) * (1 - 1/diel) * Ln(R/r)\par
\b0{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
\lang1033{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}L \tab\tab Value for distributed inductance in Henry/meter\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}diel\tab\tab Dielectric constant (as specified in the LD7 command)\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}Ln\tab\tab Natural Logarithm\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}R\tab\tab Radius of of wire plus insulation (as specified in the LD7 command)\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}r\tab\tab Radius of wire (as specified in the corresponding GW command)\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}This equation is especially suited for dielectric-constants between 2 and 3.5, the normal range of insulation materials used by radio amateurs.\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}To see the results of the internal LD conversion, use \lquote Show -> Nec 2 input\rquote  on the \lquote Geometry (F3)\rquote  window after a (4)Nec2 calculation is done or select the \lquote Show Nec\rquote  menubar option when using the \lquote Geometry editor\rquote .\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
\super{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}#$K\nosupersub\par
\pard{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlcont\pnf7\pnindent0{\pntxtb\'B7}}\nowidctlpar\ri-1800\qj\b\fs24 GN 3\tab Specifying MiniNec type ground\par
\pard{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlcont\pnf7\pnindent0{\pntxtb\'B7}}\nowidctlpar\ri-7\qj\b0\fs20\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}When \lquote connecting\rquote  wires to a Real (SomNec)- or Finite (Fast)- ground (using a Z-value of zero) the reported antenna impedance is usually unpredictable.  To avoid this, use Real/SomNec ground with an elevated radial system or, when using verticals,  use the MiniNec ground type.\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}When computing far-field data the MiniNec ground takes into account the ground conductivity and dielectric constant at a distance. However, when computing currents and antenna input- impedance it consideres the ground to be perfect. (as could be the case using a nearly perfect buried radial system).\par
\pard This ground system however is only valid when the antenna structure does not contain horizontal wires below 0.2 wavelength. This mostly restricts the use to vertical antennas. When modeling other antenna types, use an elevated radial system together with the Real/SomNec ground type.  (see {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.cebik.com "}}{\fldrslt{\cf2\ul\fs24 http://www.cebik.com/}}}\cf0\ulnone\f0\fs20  for more info about this subject).  \par
\par
The MiniNec ground type is available through the \ldblquote GN 3\rdblquote  extension on top of the default \ldblquote GN \endash 1\rdblquote  to \ldblquote GN 2\rdblquote  default NEC command set.\par
\par
\pard{\*\pn\pnlvlcont\pnf0\pnindent0\pnstart1\pndec }
\nowidctlpar\qj When using a text editor as the 4nec2 entry system the LD 6 card has the below syntax:\par
\pard{\pntext\f0 1)\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pnf0\pnindent0\pnstart1\pndec }
\nowidctlpar\qj\par
\b{\pntext\f0 2)\tab}Cmd\tab\tab I2\tab\tab I3\tab\tab I4\tab\tab Diel-const\tab Conductivity\par
\b0{\pntext\f0 3)\tab}GN\tab 3\tab\tab 0\tab\tab 0\tab\tab 0\tab\tab 14\tab\tab\tab 0.006\par
{\pntext\f0 4)\tab}\par
{\pntext\f0 5)\tab}Internally in 4nec2 this GN-type 3 is converted to a combination of perfect ground (GN 1) and a second circular cliff ground-medium (GD 0 0 0 0 diel cond). This second ground medium starts at a distance zero from the antenne and also has a height of zero.\par
{\pntext\f0 6)\tab}\par
{\pntext\f0 7)\tab}To see the results of this internal GN conversion, use \lquote Show -> Nec 2 input\rquote  on the \lquote Geometry (F3)\rquote  window after a (4)Nec2 calculation is done or select the \lquote Show Nec\rquote  menubar option when using the \lquote geometry editor\rquote .\par
{\pntext\f0 8)\tab}\par
\b{\pntext\f0 9)\tab}\line\super\b0 #$K\nosupersub\par
\pard{\*\pn\pnlvlcont\pnf0\pnindent0\pnstart1\pndec }
\nowidctlpar\ri-1800\qj\b\fs24 Using a Numerical Green\rquote s Function file (NGF)\par
\pard{\*\pn\pnlvlcont\pnf0\pnindent0\pnstart1\pndec }
\nowidctlpar\ri-7\qj\b0\fs20\par
\pard{\pntext\f0 10)\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pnf0\pnindent0\pnstart1\pndec }
\nowidctlpar\ri-7\qj Numerical Green Function files (NGF) are used to split a nec-based geometry structure in one or more a static reusable part(s) and a dynamic part. As the static part for instance one could specify a number of different refelctor screen\rquote s while the dynamic part describes the radiator. Both parts are created using standard nec input-files.\par
{\pntext\f0 11)\tab}\par
{\pntext\f0 12)\tab}For the first file, the static part, the structure is pre-calculated, thus generating a so called NGF file. This NGF file is created by adding a \lquote\b WG\b0\rquote  card to the nec input-file. \par
{\pntext\f0 13)\tab}\par
{\pntext\f0 14)\tab}In the second file, containing the more dynamic part (e.g. the radiator)  a card is added which specifies to import/include the pre-generated NGF file. This file is \lquote imported\rquote  by using the  \lquote\b GF\b0\rquote  card.\par
{\pntext\f0 15)\tab}\par
{\pntext\f0 16)\tab}Mostly the (first) input-file creating the NGF file contains far more geometry cards than the file using the NGF file. Because the NGF file is pre-generated, (re)calculation the overall structure will much more time efficient.\par
{\pntext\f0 17)\tab}\par
{\pntext\f0 18)\tab}The frequency and ground-settings to use must be specified in the first file, producing the NGF file. The second file which uses the pre-generated NGF file may not contain any FR or GN/GD cards.\par
{\pntext\f0 19)\tab}\par
{\pntext\f0 20)\tab}When using Numerical Green's Function files (NGF) some considerations must be taken into account.\par
{\pntext\f0 21)\tab}\par
{\pntext\f0 22)\tab}Using the Nec2dXS*.exe engines it's possible to explicitely specify the name of the NGF file to use. The following syntax applies:\par
{\pntext\f0 23)\tab}\par
{\pntext\f0 24)\tab}To create a NGF file\tab\tab : \b WG \i file.ext\b0\i0\par
{\pntext\f0 25)\tab}To use a NGF file\tab\tab : \b GF \i file.ext\b0\i0\par
{\pntext\f0 26)\tab}\par
{\pntext\f0 27)\tab}'file.ext' can by any file-name like 'ngf.nec' or 'screen.ngf' or 'refl.ngf'. When no NGF file-name is specified the name defaults to 'NGF2X.NEC'. These files are placed in your '..\\4nec2\\exe' folder.\par
{\pntext\f0 28)\tab}\par
{\pntext\f0 29)\tab}When a NGF file-name is specified it's also possible to use the 'Print table of coordinates' flag. The following syntax applies:\par
{\pntext\f0 30)\tab}\par
{\pntext\f0 31)\tab}To print a coord-table\tab\tab : \b GF 1 \i file.ext\b0\i0\par
{\pntext\f0 32)\tab}Don't print coordinates\tab : \b GF 0 \i file.ext\b0\i0\par
{\pntext\f0 33)\tab}\par
{\pntext\f0 34)\tab}Furthermore note that when using/importing a NGF file, 4nec2 is not able anymore to automatically detect the specified number of segments from the *.NEC input-file. To let 4nec2 select the appropriate nec2dXS*.exe engine it is needed to manually set the total number of segments.\par
{\pntext\f0 35)\tab}\par
{\pntext\f0 36)\tab}This is done with 'Setting -> Nec-engine -> Min-nr segments'. You can enter a value just above the total number of segments specified in both *.nec files. (the one generating the NGF file and the one using the NGF file)\par
{\pntext\f0 37)\tab}\par
{\pntext\f0 38)\tab}Also because ground parameters are specified in the binary *.NGF file and thus are not directly available through the second *.NEC file it is required to use an appropriate GE (-)1 or GE 0 specification in the second *.NEC file so 4nec2 knows if it has to generate a half (180 degree) or a full (360 degree) far field pattern.\par
\b{\pntext\f0 39)\tab}\par
\super{\pntext\f0 40)\tab}#$K\nosupersub\b0\par
\pard{\*\pn\pnlvlcont\pnf0\pnindent0\pnstart1\pndec }
\nowidctlpar\ri-1800\qj\b\fs24 Symbols\tab\tab Declaration of symbols/variables\par
\pard{\*\pn\pnlvlcont\pnf0\pnindent0\pnstart1\pndec }
\nowidctlpar\ri-432\qj\b0\fs20\par
\pard{\pntext\f0 41)\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pnf0\pnindent0\pnstart1\pndec }
\nowidctlpar\ri-432\qj In version 4.0 and later versions, variables, also known as symbols (especially for those using Antenna Optimizer by Brian Beezley) were introduced.\par
{\pntext\f0 42)\tab}\par
{\pntext\f0 43)\tab}With this feature you can assign a certain numeric value or numeric expression to a (character) variable. For instance you may set the variable 'len' to 20, and use this variable anywhere in your 4nec2 input-file where you want to specify a length of 20.\par
{\pntext\f0 44)\tab}\par
{\pntext\f0 45)\tab}This is done by including a special card in your 4nec2 input file with the \ldblquote SY\rdblquote  identifier.  For instance, if you want to specify a length of 20, a height of 10 and a width =0.1, you specify the following: \par
{\pntext\f0 46)\tab}\par
\pard{\*\pn\pnlvlcont\pnf0\pnindent0\pnstart1\pndec }
\nowidctlpar\li380\ri-432\qj SY len=20, hgh=10, width=0.1\par
\pard{\*\pn\pnlvlcont\pnf0\pnindent0\pnstart1\pndec }
\nowidctlpar\ri-432\qj\par
\pard{\pntext\f0 47)\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pnf0\pnindent0\pnstart1\pndec }
\nowidctlpar\ri-432\qj You have to include the SY card before using the variable in one of the succeeding NEC cards.\par
{\pntext\f0 48)\tab}\par
{\pntext\f0 49)\tab}Furthermore you may include expressions/equations when declaring variables.  For instance: \par
{\pntext\f0 50)\tab}\par
\pard{\*\pn\pnlvlcont\pnf0\pnindent0\pnstart1\pndec }
\nowidctlpar\li380\ri-432\qj SY len=10, height = 5+10*len\tab\tab\lquote  Height is 5; increased with 10 times length \par
\pard{\*\pn\pnlvlcont\pnf0\pnindent0\pnstart1\pndec }
\nowidctlpar\ri-432\qj\par
\pard{\pntext\f0 51)\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pnf0\pnindent0\pnstart1\pndec }
\nowidctlpar\ri-432\qj Valid operators in these are  + ,  - ,  * ,  / and  ^ \par
{\pntext\f0 52)\tab}\par
{\pntext\f0 53)\tab}You may also include functions like sin", "cos", "tan", "atn", "sqr", "exp", "log", "log10", "abs", "sgn", "fix", "int" and "mod". For instance: \par
{\pntext\f0 54)\tab}\par
\pard{\*\pn\pnlvlcont\pnf0\pnindent0\pnstart1\pndec }
\nowidctlpar\li380\ri-432\qj\lang1031 SY ang=60, len=20, lenX = len*cos(ang), lenY = len*sin(ang)\par
\pard{\*\pn\pnlvlcont\pnf0\pnindent0\pnstart1\pndec }
\nowidctlpar\ri-432\qj\par
\pard{\pntext\f0 55)\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pnf0\pnindent0\pnstart1\pndec }
\nowidctlpar\ri-432\qj\lang1033 Or even\par
{\pntext\f0 56)\tab}\par
{\pntext\f0 57)\tab}\tab SY dist=30mm, diam=1.3mm, Z0 = 276 * log10 ( (2*Dist) / Diam)\par
{\pntext\f0 58)\tab}\par
{\pntext\f0 59)\tab}\par
{\pntext\f0 60)\tab}Standard scientific precedence rules apply. If they must be overruled you can use brackets.\par
{\pntext\f0 61)\tab}\par
{\pntext\f0 62)\tab}Below operators are also included, however I did not yet find any real use for it:\par
{\pntext\f0 63)\tab}\par
\pard{\*\pn\pnlvlcont\pnf0\pnindent0\pnstart1\pndec }
\nowidctlpar\fi380\ri-432\qj "and", "or", "not", "xor", "eqv" and "<", ">"\rquote , "<=", "<="\par
\pard{\*\pn\pnlvlcont\pnf0\pnindent0\pnstart1\pndec }
\nowidctlpar\ri-432\qj\par
\pard{\pntext\f0 64)\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pnf0\pnindent0\pnstart1\pndec }
\nowidctlpar\ri-432\qj You may divide your variable declarations over one or more SY cards. The SY cards can be included anywhere in your input file as long as the variables are declared before they are used. You can then use these variables and/or equations in any NEC card you should like. \par
{\pntext\f0 65)\tab}\par
{\pntext\f0 66)\tab}Variable declarations have to be separated by comma's (",").\par
{\pntext\f0 67)\tab}\par
{\pntext\f0 68)\tab}A couple of fixed pre-defined identifiers are also included. They are: \par
{\pntext\f0 69)\tab}\par
\pard{\*\pn\pnlvlcont\pnf0\pnindent0\pnstart1\pndec }
\nowidctlpar\li380\ri-432\qj cm \tab\tab\tab\tab (centimeter)\par
\pard{\pntext\f0 70)\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pnf0\pnindent0\pnstart1\pndec }
\nowidctlpar\li380\ri-432\qj mm \tab\tab\tab (millimeter)\par
{\pntext\f0 71)\tab}in \tab\tab\tab\tab (inch)\par
{\pntext\f0 72)\tab}ft \tab\tab\tab\tab (feet)\par
{\pntext\f0 73)\tab}pF, nF, uF, nH \tab (pico-, nano and micro Farad)\par
{\pntext\f0 74)\tab}nH and uH \tab\tab (nano- and micro Henry). \par
\pard{\*\pn\pnlvlcont\pnf0\pnindent0\pnstart1\pndec }
\nowidctlpar\ri-432\qj\par
\pard{\pntext\f0 75)\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pnf0\pnindent0\pnstart1\pndec }
\nowidctlpar\ri-432\qj Note that the variables and identifiers are not case sensitive.\par
{\pntext\f0 76)\tab}\par
{\pntext\f0 77)\tab}When variables are used in two or more 4nec2 input-files it is possible to declare them only once in a separate definition-file. Use \lquote Settings -> predefined symbols\rquote  to view, add ,delete or modify these predefined symbols. This file could be used to declare, for instance, AWG # wire gauges, your default ground parameters and/or wire conductivity values. \par
{\pntext\f0 78)\tab}\par
{\pntext\f0 79)\tab}Prefdefined variables (constants) can be \lquote overwritten\rquote  in \lquote SY lines\rquote  in the 4nec2 input file.\par
\pard{\*\pn\pnlvlcont\pnf0\pnindent0\pnstart1\pndec }
\nowidctlpar\ri-1800\qj\par
\pard{\pntext\f0 80)\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pnf0\pnindent0\pnstart1\pndec }
\nowidctlpar\ri-1800\qj\b Remark\b0 : Please do not use the above mentioned pre-defined identifiers or functions as SYmbol names because this will lead to unpredictable results.\par
\pard{\*\pn\pnlvlcont\pnf0\pnindent0\pnstart1\pndec }
\nowidctlpar\qj\super #$K\nosupersub\par
\pard{\*\pn\pnlvlcont\pnf0\pnindent0\pnstart1\pndec }
\nowidctlpar\ri-1080\qj\b\fs24 Modeling guide-lines\par
\pard{\pntext\f0 81)\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pnf0\pnindent0\pnstart1\pndec }
\nowidctlpar\ri-1080\qj\b0\fs20\par
{\pntext\f0 82)\tab}Although most of the below described guide-lines are (automatically) checked when loading or generating new output-data or by using the \uldb structure validation\ulnone\v validate\v0  option it is suggested to keep the below modeling guide-lines in mind when creating new or modifying existing models.\par
\pard{\*\pn\pnlvlcont\pnf0\pnindent0\pnstart1\pndec }
\nowidctlpar\ri-1800\qj\par
\pard{\*\pn\pnlvlcont\pnf0\pnindent0\pnstart1\pndec }
\nowidctlpar\ri-1800\ul Wire modeling\par
\pard{\pntext\f0 83)\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pnf0\pnindent0\pnstart1\pndec }
\nowidctlpar\ri-1800\par
\ulnone{\pntext\f0 84)\tab}Len \tab = segment length\par
{\pntext\f0 85)\tab}Rad \tab = segment radius (diameter/2)\par
{\pntext\f0 86)\tab}WL \tab\tab = Wave Length\par
{\pntext\f0 87)\tab}\par
{\pntext\f0 88)\tab}1)\tab Positive current flows from end-1 to end-2\par
\pard{\*\pn\pnlvlcont\pnf0\pnindent0\pnstart1\pndec }
\nowidctlpar\ri-900\par
\pard{\*\pn\pnlvlcont\pnf0\pnindent0\pnstart1\pndec }
\nowidctlpar\ri-1800 2)\tab len < WL/10 \tab for accuracy in most cases\par
\pard{\*\pn\pnlvlcont\pnf0\pnindent0\pnstart1\pndec }
\nowidctlpar\fi380\ri-1800 len < WL/20 \tab in critical regions\par
\pard{\pntext\f0 89)\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pnf0\pnindent0\pnstart1\pndec }
\nowidctlpar\fi380\ri-1800 len < WL/5 \tab\tab on long straight segments\par
\pard{\*\pn\pnlvlcont\pnf0\pnindent0\pnstart1\pndec }
\nowidctlpar\ri-1800\par
\pard{\pntext\f0 90)\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pnf0\pnindent0\pnstart1\pndec }
\nowidctlpar\ri-1800 3)\tab Avoid extremely short segments (len < .0001 WL)\par
\pard{\*\pn\pnlvlcont\pnf0\pnindent0\pnstart1\pndec }
\nowidctlpar\li360\ri-1800\par
\pard{\*\pn\pnlvlcont\pnf0\pnindent0\pnstart1\pndec }
\nowidctlpar\ri-1800 4)\tab\lquote rad\rquote  must be small relative to \lquote len\rquote  and \lquote WL\rquote\par
\pard{\*\pn\pnlvlcont\pnf0\pnindent0\pnstart1\pndec }
\nowidctlpar\li360\ri-1800\par
\pard{\pntext\f0 91)\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pnf0\pnindent0\pnstart1\pndec }
\nowidctlpar\li360\ri-1800 rad < len/2\tab\tab with default thin wire kernel\par
{\pntext\f0 92)\tab}rad < 2*len\tab\tab with extended thin wire kernel\par
{\pntext\f0 93)\tab}rad < len/10\tab\tab default usage\par
\pard{\*\pn\pnlvlcont\pnf0\pnindent0\pnstart1\pndec }
\nowidctlpar\ri-1800\par
\pard{\pntext\f0 94)\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pnf0\pnindent0\pnstart1\pndec }
\nowidctlpar\ri-1800 5)\tab Avoid - Large changes in radius, especially on short segments\par
\pard{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnf7\pnindent360{\pntxtb\'B7}}\nowidctlpar\fi-375\li1515\ri-1800\tx1515 Sharp bends in thick wires\par
\pard{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlcont\pnf7\pnindent0{\pntxtb\'B7}}\nowidctlpar\ri-1800\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}6)\tab Wires that are connected must contact at segment ends\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\tab (connection separation < len/1000)\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}7)\tab Use equal source segment length and lengths for segments next to source\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
\pard{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlcont\pnf7\pnindent0{\pntxtb\'B7}}\nowidctlpar\fi-360\li360\ri-1800\tx360 8)\tab No voltage sources (EX 0) or loads at wire open ends\par
\pard{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlcont\pnf7\pnindent0{\pntxtb\'B7}}\nowidctlpar\ri-1800\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}9)\tab Use same segmentation for close parallel wires.\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
\pard{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlcont\pnf7\pnindent0{\pntxtb\'B7}}\nowidctlpar\fi-360\li360\ri-1800\tx360 9)\tab GE (Geometry End) usage\par
\pard{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlcont\pnf7\pnindent0{\pntxtb\'B7}}\nowidctlpar\fi380\li340\ri-1800 GE 0 \tab :\tab No ground (free space)\par
\pard{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlcont\pnf7\pnindent0{\pntxtb\'B7}}\nowidctlpar\fi380\li320\ri-1800 GE 1 \tab :\tab Ground plane present, \lquote touch\rquote  wires (Z=0) are connected to ground\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}GE -1\tab : \tab Ground plane present, \lquote touch\rquote  wires (Z=0) are insulated from ground\ul\par
\pard{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlcont\pnf7\pnindent0{\pntxtb\'B7}}\nowidctlpar\ri-1800\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}Wire grid modeling\par
\ulnone{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}1)\tab Grid-size is not too critical (appr. WL/10 at midband)\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}2)\tab Len/rad not critical (10 < len/rad < 30 goor for wires attaching surface)\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}3)\tab Minimize \lquote len\rquote  and \lquote rad\rquote  changes (<2:1) at connection pts of wires and surfaces\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}4)\tab Use denser gridding (2*) at connection points of wires and surfaces\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}5)\tab Also acceptable for thin structures (plates, wings, etc) \par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}6)\tab Try two segments/side\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}7)\tab Not usable for surface charges or currents\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
\ul{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}Surface patch modeling\par
\ulnone{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}1)\tab Make sure surface is closed and not too thin (no plates, fins or wings)\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}2)\tab Maximim patch size may not exceed 0.2 * 0.2 WL\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}3)\tab Corners are defined using right hand rule.\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}4)\tab Avoid long thin/narrow pathes\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}5)\tab Large patches on smooth surfaces, smaller ones on curved areas\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}6)\tab Currents are defined at centre only, so not good for edge currents\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}8)\tab When wires are connected they must do so at patch centers\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}9)\tab Use \lquote wire-grids\rquote  where edge connections are needed. \par
\pard{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlcont\pnf7\pnindent0{\pntxtb\'B7}}\nowidctlpar\li360\ri-450 - Wire grid equals surface if mesh is \lquote small enough\rquote\par
\pard{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlcont\pnf7\pnindent0{\pntxtb\'B7}}\nowidctlpar\li360\ri-1800 - Problem:\par
\pard{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlcont\pnf7\pnindent0{\pntxtb\'B7}}\nowidctlpar\li735\ri-1800 - can\rquote t afford real fine meshes (*\par
\pard{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlcont\pnf7\pnindent0{\pntxtb\'B7}}\nowidctlpar\fi380\li355\ri-1800 - Sparse meshes have too much L, not enough C\par
\pard{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlcont\pnf7\pnindent0{\pntxtb\'B7}}\nowidctlpar\li355\ri-1800 - Possible solutions:\par
\pard{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlcont\pnf7\pnindent0{\pntxtb\'B7}}\nowidctlpar\li760\ri-1800 - negative L distributed loading\par
\pard{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlcont\pnf7\pnindent0{\pntxtb\'B7}}\nowidctlpar\fi380\li380\ri-1800 - fat, rod-like wires\par
\pard{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlcont\pnf7\pnindent0{\pntxtb\'B7}}\nowidctlpar\ri-432\qj\up10\fs13\par
\pard{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlcont\pnf7\pnindent0{\pntxtb\'B7}}\nowidctlpar\ri-1800\qj\super #$K\nosupersub\par
\b\up0\fs28{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}Calculate\tab\tab F7\tab\tab Generate NEC output-data\par
\pard{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlcont\pnf7\pnindent0{\pntxtb\'B7}}\nowidctlpar\ri-360\qj\b0\fs20\par
\pard{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlcont\pnf7\pnindent0{\pntxtb\'B7}}\nowidctlpar\ri-630\qj This general option is used for generating new NEC output-data.\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}If one of the below described options is selected, first of all, 4nec2 pre-processes the active *.nec input file to remove all comment and to solve the variable- and constant definitions. The resulting intermediate file, containing pure NEC input-data is placed in the \ldblquote\\out\rdblquote  folder with the *.inp extension. When this is done, the Nec-2 or Nec-4 engine is started and the *.inp file is red by the engine to generate new NEC output-data. This output-data is placed as an *.out output-file in the \ldblquote\\out\rdblquote  folder.  When all calculations are done 4nec2 open this output-file and the results are displayed on the F2 to F5 forms.\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}When a Sommerfeld-Norton ground is specified, 4nec2 automatically generates or copies the appropriate ground file to be used by the Nec-2 engine. These ground files (with the *.gnd extension) are stored in the \ldblquote\\out\rdblquote  folder. If the requested file already exists, it is copied; otherwise a new one is created with the somnec engine. When using the Nec2dXS engine the SomNec data is calculated by the engine itself and the additional somnec engine and ground files are not required.\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}When the \lquote Generate\rquote  command-button is selected, a pop-up window is displayed with different options to choose from.\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
\ul\b{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}The following generate options are available:\par
\ulnone\b0{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}Use original file\tab Run NEC-engine and generate new output from active *.nec input file\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}Far field pattern\tab Run NEC and generate specified 3D far field pattern for input file \par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\tab Full\tab\tab\tab Generate full 3D far-field pattern.\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\tab Ver.\tab\tab\tab Generate vertical far-field pattern for specified phi angle.\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\tab Hor.\tab\tab\tab Generate horizontal far-field pattern for specified theta angle.\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}Frequency sweep Run NEC and request for calculations for a range of frequencies \par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\tab Gain.\tab\tab Run NEC and generate SWR and Gain graphs for specified phi and theta.\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\tab Ver.\tab\tab\tab Generate vertical far-field pattern for each frequency and SWR and Gain graphs.\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\tab Hor.\tab\tab\tab Generate horizontal far-field pattern for each frequency and SWR and Gain graphs.\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\tab Full\tab\tab\tab Generate 3D far-field pattern to get full Gain, F/B- and F/R-ratio data for graphs.\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}Near field pattern\tab Run NEC and generate specified near field pattern\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\tab E-fld\tab\tab Generate near-electric-field data and show in 2/3D pattern and graph style. \par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\tab H-fld\tab\tab Generate near-magnetic-field data and show in 2/3D pattern and graph style.\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}360 degree Gain table\tab Generate ItsHF 360 * 90 degree Gain-table for specified frequency\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}Gain @ 30 frequencies\tab Generate ItsHF 90 degree Gain-tables for 30 frequencies\par
\b{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
\b0{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}Batch (button)\tab To run multiple files as a single batch, see below.\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}More (button)\tab To define more settings.Visible when one of both ItsHF options is seleceted.\par
\b{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}Use Original file\par
\b0{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}When this option is used, the 4nec2 input file is converted to default NEC format and send directly to the NEC-engine, without further modifications and/or additions. You can use this option for a quick first test after creating a new nec file or to specify calculation options not supported by 4nec2.\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
\i{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}When using one of the other (below) options it is recommended to remove all RP and NE/NH cards from the *.nec input file. \par
\b\i0{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}Far-field pattern\par
\b0{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}The most used calculation probably will be the default far-field pattern calculation. This pattern determines in which direction you antenna system will radiate/receive best and in which direction signal is supressed. It may also be used for signal-coverage studies and interference-level analysis. \par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}When the default far-field (full, horizontal or vertical) pattern is requested one must specify the resolution to use. This resolution value defines the stepsize increments for the Phi/Azimuth and Theta/Elevation angles. \par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}When a ground(plane) is involved, only the upper halve of the \lquote radiation-sphere\rquote  is calculated. When low resolution (< 1 deg.) is specified it is possible that the max-nr of field-points is exceeded. If so, use the \lquote ver\rquote  or \lquote hor\rquote  option, or increase the max-nr of far-field points in the settings menu.\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}When running a Full/3D far field pattern, by default the radiation-efficiency and the Receiving Directivity Factor (RDF) are also calculated and displayed on the Main (F2) window. RDF is specified as the difference between (maximum) forward-gain of an antenna and the calculated average-gain of the antenna. For more info, see \ul Radiation-efficiency\ulnone\v Radeff\v0 . \par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}Note that when running a horizontal/vertical 2D pattern or when a surface-wave request is made, no radiation-efficiency is calculated.\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}On the middle-right of the window one can specify if an average-gain-test (AGT) should be performed. With this you can check if your antenna model is more or less a valid Nec2/4 model. See \ul Average Gain-Test\ulnone\v Gain\v0  for additional info about the subject. Running an average-gain test is only possible if the full/3D far-field option (without surface wave) is selected.\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}Experienced NEC users can use the \lquote expert\rquote  button to set special far-field calculating options. For additional info about these \lquote XNDA\rquote  options, please consult the Nec-2 or Nec-4 user-guide. Default XNDA settings are:\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}1:\tab Ver/Hor/total field, \par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}0:\tab No normalization\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}0:\tab Power-gain\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}2:\tab Compute average-gain\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}If required, \lquote\uldb Auto-segmentation\ulnone\v Aseg\v0\rquote  or \lquote\uldb Step-radius correction\ulnone\v Srad\v0\rquote  can be enabled through the \lquote\uldb Setttings\ulnone\v settings\v0\rquote  menu.\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}When the far-field option is selected, besides the Gain values the default E-field is calculated. If required, one can check the \ldblquote E-fld\rdblquote  box. If done so, one may enter a certain distance (in Km) for which the E-field is calculated. (Use \ldblquote <\rdblquote  and \ldblquote >\rdblquote  on the Pattern [F4] window to switch between Gain and E-field values).\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}By default only the space-wave and the ground-refelected wave are used for far-field calculations. To include the surface-wave in the calculations, check the \ldblquote Surf-wave\rdblquote  box. If this is used, additional parameters are required:\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}-\tab Max-Z\tab\tab Maximum height (meter) for which the far-field is calculated.\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}-\tab Dist\tab\tab\tab Distance for which the far-field is calculated, mostly set to 1 Km\ul\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
\ulnone\b{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}Frequency sweep\par
\b0{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
\pard{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlcont\pnf7\pnindent0{\pntxtb\'B7}}\ri-630\qj When a frequency-sweep is requested (starting with version 5.8.0) on can choose between two further options:\f3\par
\f0{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\~\f3\par
\f0{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}a) Sweep by specifying a start- and stop frequency together with a fixed step-size (delta frequency)\f3\par
\f0{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}b) Sweep using a predefined list of frequencies. If this is required, the \lquote\ul from file\ulnone\rquote  box should be checked.\f3\par
\f0{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\~\f3\par
\f0{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}Starting with version 5.8.0 one can let 4nec2 show you the change in current-distribution by \lquote walking\rquote  throught the list of calculated frequencies. For futher info see the \uldb Geometry\ulnone  window.\f3\par
\f0{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\~\f3\par
\f0{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}When an \b a)\b0  type frequency-sweep is requested, additional Start, Stop and Step-size data have to be specified. Do not specify a Start (and Stop) frequency very much apart from the design frequency, otherwise certain NEC requirements, such as segments per wavelength, might be violated. \f3\par
\f0{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\~\f3\par
\pard{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlcont\pnf7\pnindent0{\pntxtb\'B7}}\nowidctlpar\ri-630\qj\f0 To evaluate the performance over a wide range of frequencies you could consider using the \uldb Sweeper\ulnone\v Eval\v0  function with \uldb Auto-segmentation\ulnone\v Aseg\v0  enabled. \par
\pard{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlcont\pnf7\pnindent0{\pntxtb\'B7}}\ri-630\qj\~\f3\par
\f0{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}With the first option, called \lquote Gain\rquote  you must supply the Phi/Theta or Azimuth/Elevation Forward-angle\~ for which a Gain-graph should be plotted. Because only one far-field point is calculated (that for the specified direction) no F/B-ratio and F/R-ration data is available or displayed.\f3\par
\f0{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\~\f3\par
\f0{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}To get F/B (front-to-back) or F/R (front-to-rear) plots; select one of the other options. Ver(tical)-, Hor(izontal)- or 3D-pattern. Using the \lquote Ver\rquote  and \lquote Hor\rquote  options, for each frequency step, a complete vertical- or horizontal-pattern is calculated for the specified Theta/Elev or Phi/Azim angle. \f3\par
\f0{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}Furthermore the F/B and F/R ratio is calculated and plotted on the \lquote Line-chart (F5)\rquote  form. These ratios are calculated for the selected plane. To get F/B and F/R-ratio calculated in 3D space (as generally used), select the 3D option.\f3\par
\f0{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\~\f3\par
\f0{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}Note that none-directional antenna systems don\rquote t produce meaningful F/B- and/or F/R-ratio data. \f3\par
\f0{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\~\f3\par
\f0{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}Note also that when using the \lquote Full\rquote  option only the whole 3D pattern for the last frequency-step is displayed on the \lquote Patten (F4)\rquote  form. This to limit the overall memory usage for the 4nec2 package. To visualize multiple patterns, select the \lquote hor\rquote  or \lquote ver\rquote  option. Use the arrow-keys to visualize the changing of the pattern (play-back) for the specified frequency-range. \f3\par
\f0{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\~\f3\par
\f0{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}When specifying the main-beam direction the backward direction is automatically updated, according the selected option (ver/hor or 3D) and the free-space/ground-plane settings. You may overwrite these defaults manually with your own settings.\f3\par
\f0{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\~\f3\par
\f0{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}When the \b b)\b0  type frequency-sweep is requested one can specify the list of frequencies to use by selecting \lquote Settings -> pre-defined frequencies\rquote  on the \lquote Main (F2)\rquote  window. Be sure to specify the list of frequencies with increasing frequency values, else you will get a somewhat corrupted \lquote line-chart\rquote  display. \f3\par
\pard{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlcont\pnf7\pnindent0{\pntxtb\'B7}}\nowidctlpar\ri-630\qj\ul\f0\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}Gain calculation\par
\ulnone{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}By default forward-gain is calculated as the single gain-value for the specified direction. F/B ratio is calculated as the difference in gain between the forward- and backward-directions. The F/R-ratio is calculated as the difference between the forward-gain and the maximum gain-value for the backward half of the radiation pattern which is centered around the backward-direction.\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}To get more control over how forward-gain and F/B-ratio are calculated (especially usefull when optimizing) it is possible to specify delta-theta and -phi values for both the forward- and backward-direction. If a none zero value is specified the gain over the specified region as defined by the Theta/Phi value plus or minus the specified delta value is averaged. The number of averaging steps is determined by the specified resolution.\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
\ul{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}Sommerfeld ground\par
\ulnone{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}When using the Real/SomNec ground in a frequency-sweep in conjunction with de default nec2d engine, the \lquote negative conductivity trick\rquote  is used, in which the Real/SomNec ground data is generated only once for all frequencies.  However this Nec-2 behavour will generate less precise data. \par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}To overcome this \lquote problem\rquote  one could use the \uldb Sweeper\ulnone\v Eval\v0  function and specify the frequency as a variable or you could select the enhanced nec2dXS engine. This modified Nec-2 engine recalculates the Real/SomNec ground data for each frequency-step, so precision is not degraded.\par
\ul{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}Troubleshooting\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
\ulnone{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}During generation of near/far-fields, frequency-sweep- or ItsHF-data, additional NEC card's are added to the original *.nec input file. If error's are generated during calculation, you may inspect this temporary NEC input-file by selecting \lquote View -> Last NEC input\rquote  on the \lquote Geometry (F3)\rquote  form, just after performing the (failed) calculation. Use the F8 function-key to inspect NEC output-file data. \par
\ul{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
\ulnone\b{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}Near-field pattern\par
\b0{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}The field close to the antenna is called the near-field. This field is quite different from the far-field that characterizes propagated electromagnetic waves. Near-field intensities are very usefull for EMI studies, RFI and TVI minimization or FR-hazard analysis.\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}In contrast with the far-field, the near-field is calculated over a 3D grid of points. Start, Stop and Step-size values for the three dimensions X, Y and/or Z have to be specified. Mostly a step-size of (Stop \endash  Start) / 20 will be fine. You can select between the electric or magnetic component of the near field to be computed. \par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}Use the \lquote test\rquote  button to let 4nec2 automatically create start-, stop- and stepsize-settings for a 3D volume which includes the whole geometry structure. This to quickly create initial near-field patterns which can then be used to further fine-tune the start-, stop- and stepsize settings. \par
\b{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}ItsHF option\par
\b0{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}With version 5.6.0 and later a more user-friendly interface with the ItsHF software has been created. The new interface enables you to create two different types of ItsHF compatible Antenne input-files:\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}- Full 360 degree Gain table for sepcified/design frequency for the antenne under test (\uldb type 13\ulnone\v type13\v0 )\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}- Set of 2D gain tables for a specified Phi/Azimuth angle for frequencies from 2 to 30 Mhz (\uldb type 14\ulnone\v type14\v0 )\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}Both types of antenna files (and other pre-defined ItsHF types) can then be used to create Area-coverage or  Point-to-point plots. After the antenna files are created one can let 4nec2 automatically create 4 kinds of plots:\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}- Area-coverage map\tab\tab\tab Plot showing propagation performance on a (partial) world-map.\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}- Point-to-point time plot\tab\tab Chart showing propagation performance as function of time\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}- Point-to-point distance plot\tab Chart showing propagation performance as function of distance\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}- Point-to-point graph plot\tab\tab Chart showing propagation performance as function of time and frequency\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}Above \lquote propagation performance\rquote  can be visualized using user selectable parameters like \lquote Signal strength at receiver\rquote , \ldblquote Signal to Noise ratio\rquote , \lquote Maximum usable frequency\rquote  or \lquote Radiation angle\rquote  \par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}Besides transmitter- and receiver-antennas and -locations, the ItsHF propagation prediction software requires additional settings to automatically create the requested plot. These parameters, settings and options are discussed under \uldb ItsHF propagation prediction\ulnone\v itshf\v0 . \par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}To use the ItsHF software package it must be installed on your computer system. Use '\uldb Settings\ulnone\v settings\v0  -> Folders' to specify your ItsHF home folder.\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
\super\b{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}#$K\nosupersub\b0\par
\b{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}Batch mode\par
\b0{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}With version 5.4.1 running 4nec2 in batch-mode was added. This way one is able to run 4nec2 for a complete set of 4nec2, Antenna-optimizer or EZnec input-files as a single batch of work.\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}To use this feature, Open the \ldblquote Generate (F7)\rdblquote  form/window and select the required calculation option. After this, click the \ldblquote Batch\rdblquote  button at the bottom of the window. If done so, a new \ldblquote Batch processing\rdblquote  form/window is displayed. To add files to the list, select the required files using Windows-Explorer (with Ctrl- and Shift key\rquote s), drag them to the Batch-processing window (while holding down the left-mouse button) and drop them onto the window.  All selected files are now automatically added to the list of files to process. You can add a single file using the \ldblquote Add\rdblquote  button. To delete one or more files, select these files using the left mouse-button and click the \ldblquote Del\rdblquote  button. To select (and delete) all files use Ctrl+A.\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}When all required files are added, click the \ldblquote Start\rdblquote  button to let 4nec2 process all listed files. In the column labeled \ldblquote Status\rdblquote  the outcome of the calculation and geometry/segment validation is logged.\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}To (temprorarely) halt batch processing of input-files, click the \ldblquote Halt\rdblquote  button and wait till the NEC calculations for the current file are done. If you think this will take too long, you can alternatively abort the NEC calculations by clicking/selecting the NEC-engine in the windows task-bar and clicking the close button of it. To resume batch processing use the \ldblquote Start after last\rdblquote  menu-option.\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}When all files are calculated, depending on the \ldblquote File -> Refresh 4Nec2 data\rdblquote  option you can move the focus or double-click on any of the listed files to let 4nec2 immediately load and display the data for the selected file.\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}Click on the \lquote File\rquote  column-header to sort all file-names in ascending or descending order.\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}Batch processing can also be used to convert multiple antenna-optimizer (*.ant) or Eznec (*.ez) input-files into Nec based files.\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
\b{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
\super{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}#$K\nosupersub\b0\par
\b{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}Command-line mode\par
\b0{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}With version 5.7.3, none-interactive command-line mode was added. This way one can start 4nec2 from a windows batch file (*.bat) and let 4nec2 read the specified 4nec2 input-file, pre-process the data, run the Nec2/4 calculation engine and create a Nec output-file.\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}To create usefull far- or near-field data one must add his/her own RP, NE or NH command in the $nec2 input-file to be used. One can use the \lquote use original-file option\rquote  on the \ldblquote Calculate (F7)\rdblquote  to check the results of the RP, NE or NH card specified.\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}To use 4nec2 in command-line mode, create (for instance) a test.bat file (in the same folder where the input-files are listed) and use below syntax:\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\ldblquote C:\\4nec2\\exe\\4nec2.exe <file1.nec> -I\rdblquote\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\ldblquote C:\\4nec2\\exe\\4nec2.exe <file2.nec> -I\rdblquote\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\ldblquote C:\\4nec2\\exe\\4nec2.exe <file3.nec> -I\rdblquote\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}etc\'85\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}When the \ldblquote -I\rdblquote  argument is not used, 4nec2 does start in \lquote normal\rquote  interactive mode with the specified input-file.\par
\super\b{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}#$K\nosupersub\b0\par
\b\fs24{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}Auto-segmentation\par
\b0\f3\fs20{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
\f0{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}When optimizing and/or sweeping antenna performance, it is likely that dimensions and/or frequencies are changing, so it is possible that certain NEC requirements, such as the minimum number of segments per half-wave are not met any more. To overcome this problem, Auto-Segmentation is used. \par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}If this feature is enabled (See \uldb Settings\ulnone\v settings\v0  on Main window), each calculation/optimization/sweeping step, the wire length for each wire is checked against the actual wave-length and the specified number of segments per wire, and the position for voltage/current sources, RLC-loading and transmission lines is updated.  \par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}If a certain number of segments is already specified in the input file, at least this number of segments is set with auto-segmentation. This to avoid auto-segmentation decrease the number of segments specified for critical regions.  If you do not want to specify a minimum, set the number of segments to one.\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}If the number of segments is preceded by a \ldblquote +\rdblquote  sign the number of segments is fixed, meaning, the segments for this wire are not affected by the auto- segmentation process.\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}When enabling the Auto-Segmentation feature, you are asked to specify the number of segments per half-wave. Although, mostly accuracy improves if more segments are specified, also the computation time is increased. For \lquote simple\rquote  structures, without sharp angles, a value from 10 to 20 will be sufficient. If precise impedance values are required or if wires joining at sharp angles are used more segments could be required. If you are not sure what amount to specify, please use increasing values and compare the results. (You could do this using the \uldb Sweeper\ulnone\v eval\v0  function). Also abrupt current changes on the structure might indicate an insufficient number of segments. One place more segments aren\rquote t better is if wires of different diameters are connected.\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}If computer performance is an issue, you could consider not to the use the auto-segmentation feature. If this feature is disabled, the processing especially for large input files with many wires is speed-up.\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
\super\b{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}#$K\nosupersub\b0\par
\b\fs24{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}Step-radius correction\par
\b0\fs20{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}When modeling Yagis or other antennas with stepped diameter wires\i ,\i0  4nec2 can perform an automatic stepped radius (stepped diameter) correction. \par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}If this feature is enabled (See \uldb Settings\ulnone\v settings\v0  on Main window) a substitute element is created for each tapered one found (with certain restrictions, see \ldblquote Tapering to perfection\rdblquote  by L.B. Cebik at www.cebik.com). The substitute element has a uniform diameter and a length that yield essentially the same self-impedance as the tapered diameter element. Thus, the substitute can be expected to behave--either by itself or in an array of elements--in the same way as the tapered element. All this to minimize inaccuracy when using large radius differences on a single element.\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}It does no harm to request the use of a stepped radius correction even in cases where the correction can not be applied. However, this results in some unnecessary processing that will increase the total time needed for calculations so I suggest you not use StepRad except in cases where it is necessary. \par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}The conversion program has a limitation in that it will not apply the correction to Yagi elements unless they are parallel to either the X or Y axis. Yagis that "point" in a direction other than a multiple of 90\'b0 azimuth, or Yagis that have been oriented for vertical polarization, will not be corrected\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}In case of problems using this option, please don\rquote t hesitate to ask \ul me\ulnone\v about\v0  for support.\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
\super\f3{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}#$K\nosupersub\par
\b\i\f0\fs24{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}Average-Gain-Test\par
\b0\i0\fs20{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}To check the reliability of your model, you can run an \lquote average gain test\rquote  (AGT). To run such a test, use the \lquote Generate (F7)\rquote  command, select \lquote Far field pattern\rquote  and check \lquote Aver-Gain test\rquote . Then click the \lquote Generate\rquote  button.\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}When the \lquote expert\rquote  button is selected and using the lowest option-box, one should select the \lquote 1 \endash  average gain\rquote  option to perform this same test. (The other option-boxes could be left to 1:Ver/Hor/Tot field, 0: no norm, 0: power-gain). \par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}To get usefull test results 4nec2 automatically (temporary) removes all wire- and other resistive loading and (if specified) changes the gound-conditions to perfect-ground when doing the required calculations.  \par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}The test-results are presented on the main (F2) form. \par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}The following gradation can be made for the Average-Gain-Test results:\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}> 0.95\tab and <\tab 1.05\tab Model is likely to be accurate\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}> 0.90\tab and <\tab 1.10\tab Model is usable for most purposes.\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}> 0.80\tab and <\tab 1.20\tab Model may be useful, but can be improved.\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}< 0.80 \tab or >\tab 1.20\tab\tab Model is questionable and should be refined.\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}When the resulting value is below 0.8 or above 1.2 the value is displayed in red color. \par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}The db value between brackets can be used to correct the far-field pattern gain.\par
\super\f3{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}#$K\nosupersub\par
\b\i\f0\fs24{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}Radiation efficiency\par
\b0\i0\fs20{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}If ground-, wire- or other R(LC)-losses are involved, the value for \lquote Radiation efficiency\rquote  might be usefull data. \par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}Radiation efficiency is defined as the ratio of \lquote pure\rquote  radiated power to the antenne input-power. \par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}This data is automatically generated and displayed on the \lquote Main\rquote  form when a full/3D far-field request is made. When using the \lquote expert\rquote  one can manually decide between letting Nec2/4 calculate average-gain and thus radiation-efficiency data. To do so, set the lower option box on the \lquote Generate (F7)\rquote  form to \ldblquote 2: Radiation efficiency\rdblquote  .\par
\pard{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlcont\pnf7\pnindent0{\pntxtb\'B7}}\nowidctlpar\ri-432\qj\par
\super{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}#$K\nosupersub\par
\pard{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlcont\pnf7\pnindent0{\pntxtb\'B7}}\nowidctlpar\ri-1800\qj\b\fs24 Open file\tab\tab Ctrl+O\tab\tab Open NEC input file\par
\b0\fs20{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
\pard{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlcont\pnf7\pnindent0{\pntxtb\'B7}}\nowidctlpar\ri-432\qj This general option is used to open another input (or output) file. You may specify an alternative folder or file-extension to search for. When opening the selected file, the program first try's to locate the corresponding output file with the extension *.out. When such a file exists, this file is loaded and the data is displayed on the Main, Geometry, Pattern and Line-chart forms.\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}When the default output file is not available, the *.nec input file is loaded, pre-processed and converted to a *.inp file and the corresponding data is displayed in the Main and Geometry forms. The Pattern, Line-chart and Smith-chart forms are not available.\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}It is possible that the 4nec2 program generates an error when or after loading a default or temporary output file. This is mostly the case if the output file is incomplete, because incorrect input file data is used. If this is the case, remove the *.out file from the \ldblquote\\out\rdblquote  folder, change the *.nec input file and try again.\par
\pard{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlcont\pnf7\pnindent0{\pntxtb\'B7}}\nowidctlpar\ri-1800\qj\par
\pard{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlcont\pnf7\pnindent0{\pntxtb\'B7}}\nowidctlpar\ri-432\qj When specifying an input-file with the '.ant' extension it is assumed you selected a AO (antenna optimizer) or Nec-wires input file and you are asked if this file has to be converted to 4nec2 format. If confirmed, most of the input data is automatic translated from AO/Nec-wires format to 4nec2 format. \par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}However the following items must be checked for:\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}- The need to include the optional GX card when X-axis symmetry was specified.\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}- Additional excitation and spot-loads when the GX card is included.\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}- Excitation and spot-load placement on wire ends.\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}When specifying an input-file with the '.ez' extension it is assumed you selected an EZnec type input file and you are asked if this file has to be converted to 4nec2 format. If confirmed a new file with the same name but with an \ldblquote .nec\rdblquote  extension is created and opened. \par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}For as far as I (the author) am aware all Eznec files should automatically be converted without needing further (manual) corrections.\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab} \par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}On the internet, nec files with the slightly different Unix/Linux text-file format are available. If you would want to load such a file, this format is automatic detected by 4nec2. If so, you are asked if this file has to be converted to the default windows type text-file.\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
\pard{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlcont\pnf7\pnindent0{\pntxtb\'B7}}\nowidctlpar\ri-1800\qj\super\b #$K\nosupersub\par
\fs24{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}Save file as\tab\tab Ctrl+S\tab\tab Save output file as\par
\b0\fs20{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
\pard{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlcont\pnf7\pnindent0{\pntxtb\'B7}}\nowidctlpar\ri-432\qj This general option is used to save the active output file data to an output-file with a different name. This option is useful for comparing different far field radiation patterns or if you want to save calculation or optimizer results for later investigation. (Open file with *.out or other extension)\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}When comparing different far field patterns be sure these files are generated with the same Theta en Phi settings. If not, you won't be able to open these files.\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}If you want to compare optimizer results with manual generated results note that the optimizer only calculates far-field points for theta angles between 0 and 90 (ground) or 180 (free-space) degrees and this is only done when a resolution unequal zero is set. \par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}If you are not sure which RP card settings are used for a specific calculation or optimization cycle, open the *.out output-file (F8 key) and look for the RP settings.\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
\pard{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlcont\pnf7\pnindent0{\pntxtb\'B7}}\nowidctlpar\ri-1800\qj\super #$K\nosupersub\par
\b\fs24{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}Settings\tab\tab Modify user-, program- or memory-usage settings\par
\pard{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlcont\pnf7\pnindent0{\pntxtb\'B7}}\nowidctlpar\qj\b0\fs20\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}The Settings option on the Main form is used to modify default-, user- or program- settings. The setting under 'memory settings' are program settings. The maximum values for these settings depend on you computer configuration. \par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}The following settings are available:\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}Notepad edit\tab\tab\tab Use windows default Notepad editor to edit input-file\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}NEC editor (old)\tab\tab Use old-style NEC-syntax aware editor to edit input-file\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}Geometry edit\tab\tab Use drag-and-draw style geometry editor to edit input-file\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}NEC editor (new)\tab\tab Use new-style NEC-syntax aware editor to edit input-file\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}Auto-Segmentation\tab To enable auto-segmentation feature and specify nr of segments\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}Step-radius corr.\tab\tab To enable stepped-radius correction.\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}Show circular-polar.\tab Show circular polarization components E-left/right instead of E-theta/phi\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}Char-impedance\tab\tab To set the characteristic line impedance for SWR plot and Smith chart\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}Input-power\tab\tab\tab Input power used for voltage, current and field strength calculations\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}Phi/Azim unit\tab\tab To select between Phi/Theta and Elevation/azimuth usage.\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}Length unit\tab\tab\tab To select between meter, wavelength, feet or inch as length unit.\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}Radius unit\tab\tab\tab To select between millimeter or inch/Awg as radius unit.\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}Predefined Freq\rquote s\tab To edit the list of fixed frequencies for a \lquote from file\rquote  frequency-sweep. Predefined symbols\tab To edit your predefined SYmbols list.\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\tab\tab\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}Nec-engine:\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\tab Nec2d engine\tab\tab\tab Use Nec2d*.exe as NEC calculation engine (1)\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\tab Nec2dXS engine\tab\tab\tab Use Nec2dXS*.exe engine, especially for windows-XP systems (2)\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\tab Nec4D engine\tab\tab\tab Use Nec4D*.exe as NEC calculation engine (3)\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\tab special engine\tab\tab\tab Use special engine \par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\tab Manual select\tab\tab\tab Use \lquote min-Nr segments\rquote  to select engine-\lquote size\rquote  or specify Nec4 engine name\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\tab Min-nr segments \tab\tab To set the nec2d(XS).exe as selected by 4nec2 (see below)\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}Memory usage:\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\tab max-nr fld-points\tab\tab\tab Maximum nr of near/far field calculation points\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\tab max freq/sweep\tab\tab\tab Maximum nr of steps  for frequency/variable-sweep.\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\tab max-nr LD cards\tab\tab\tab Maximum nr of (wire) loading cards\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\tab max-nr TL cards\tab\tab\tab Maximum nr of  transmission-lines\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\tab max-nr EX cards\tab\tab\tab Maximum nr of excitation sources\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\tab max-nr 3D-points\tab\tab\tab Maximum nr of points used in 3D-viewer (graphics memory)\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\tab max-nr of SYmbols\tab\tab Maximum nr of SYmbols to use with optimizer/evoluter.\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}Optimizer settings:\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\tab Absolute steps\tab\tab\tab If selected use absolute steps else use relative steps\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\tab Max-initial step size\tab\tab Initial/maximum step size used for the optimization process in %\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\tab Step size if optimized\tab Step size for which the optimization is considered to be ready in %\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}ItsHF settings:\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\tab --- area coverage-----\tab\tab By default only the type-13 area-coverage antenna file is generated\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\tab Create \lquote Area\rquote  input-file\tab If selected create *.ICE/REC/VOA area-plot input file \par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\tab Calculate Area-plot data\tab If selected create *.IG1/RG1/VG1 plot/data-file\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\tab Show Area-plot on screen\tab If selected generate and show Area plot on the screen\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\tab --- Point-to-Point ------\tab\tab By default, only the type-14 point-to-point antenna file is generated\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\tab Create \lquote Point\rquote  input-file\tab If selected create *.ICE/REC/VOA point-to-point input file \par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\tab Calculate Point plot-data\tab If selected create time/distance/graph plot-data file\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}Show Point-plots on screen\tab If selected generate and show point-to-point plot on screen\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\tab --- engines--------------\tab\tab Select predicion model/engine to use\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\tab use ICEPAC\tab\tab\tab\tab If selected, use ICEAREA/ICEPAC model for calculation\tab\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\tab use REC533\tab\tab\tab\tab If selected, use RECAREA/REC533 model for calculation\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\tab use VOAcap\tab\tab\tab\tab If selected, use VOAREA/VOAPAC model for calculation\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}Other settings:\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\tab Rear segment size\tab\tab To specify the size of the rearward segment for F/R calculation\par
\pard{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlcont\pnf7\pnindent0{\pntxtb\'B7}}\nowidctlpar\fi380\qj Wait for DOS-box\tab\tab If checked wait in calculation engine dos-box for Enter key\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}Fast/(auto)redraw\tab\tab To select between maximum speed or automatic screen refresh\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}LIN min. value\tab\tab\tab Default minimum scale value for linear far field dB-scale\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}LOG min. value\tab\tab\tab Default minimum scale value for logarithmic far field dB-scale\par
\pard{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlcont\pnf7\pnindent0{\pntxtb\'B7}}\nowidctlpar\qj\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}Folder settings\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\tab Notepad editor\tab\tab\tab To set the path to Notepad.exe or other custom editor\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\tab Wordpad editor \tab\tab\tab To set the path to Wordpad.exe or other custom editor\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\tab ItsHF folder\tab\tab\tab\tab To set the main/home-folder for the \uldb ItsHF\ulnone\v itshf\v0  propagation prediction program\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\tab gnuplot folder\tab\tab\tab To set the main/home-folder for the \lquote\uldb gnuplot\ulnone\v IDH_gpl\v0\rquote  plotting program\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\tab NecWin+ folder\tab\tab\tab To set the main/home-folder for NecWinPlus\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
\ul{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}Input power\par
\ulnone{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}The 'power' setting is used to compute the structure currents and the magnitude for the near electric and magnetic field. The effect is that the voltage for the voltage source on the EX card is modified in such a way as if it would be perfectly matched and delivers the specified input-power to the antenna structure. (Constant power mode, (multiplication with SQRT (specified_input_power / input_power_from_output_file)) If you would like to keep the original specified voltage in the EX card, please specify an input-power of 0 (zero) watts. (Constant voltage mode)\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}Please note also that the Voltages and Currents specified in the 4nec2 program are effective values, and not maximum values as used by the NEC-engine.\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
\ul{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}Number of segments\par
\ulnone{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}The maximum number of segments that can be modelled on your system depends on the of physical memory (RAM) available on your computer. For different number of segments separate engines (requiring different amounts of memory) are selected by 4nec2. \par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}Select \lquote Help -> About 4nec2\rquote , to see the total amount of physical memory available on your system. \par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}In version 5.0 or later, 4nec2 automatically calculates the engine-\lquote size\rquote  that is required. Mostly this will be fine. The engine selected is the one specified by both the number of segments (and patches) in your model and the \lquote Min-nr of segments\rquote  setting (see below). If segment-count goes up, a \lquote larger\rquote  engine is automatically selected.\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}However in special cases when using the the NGF (numerical Green\rquote s fuction) or related to GX and GM cards, it is possible the wrong engine is selected. (an error like: \ldblquote nr of segments and surface patches exceeds dimension limit\rdblquote  may be generated). If this is the case when using a Nec-2 based engine, you could switch to \lquote manual selection\rquote . To do this, set the \lquote Manual select\rquote  option and then specify the \lquote Max-nr of segments\rquote  setting. The engine(size) used now is selected according the number of segments specified.\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}When using a licensed Nec-4 engine you can use this option to manually specify the executable file-name to use as the NEC-engine\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab} \par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}The following table gives you some indication about the available public-domain Nec2d engines, the maximum number of segments to calculate and the minimum required amount of RAM. Note however that these numbers are based on running the old DOS operating system. Because 4nec2 is based on the windows-XX system, less memory is available for the nec engine, especially when running windows-XP which uses a large amount of available memory by itself\'85\'85 \par
\pard{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlcont\pnf7\pnindent0{\pntxtb\'B7}}\nowidctlpar\ri-432\qj\par
\f4\fs16{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}                  Name of      Max number  Approximate\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}                  executable   of segm.    memory use (MB)     \par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}               - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - \par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}             /    NEC2D.EXE        256         1.5 (*)\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab} These are  |     NEC2D512.EXE     512         5\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab} found in   |     NEC2D960.EXE     960        16\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab} nec-pc.zip |     NEC2D1K4.EXE    1392        32\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}             \\    NEC2D1K9.EXE    1950        60\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}             /    NEC2D2K8.EXE    2800       122\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}These files |     NEC2D4K.EXE     4000       247\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}  are in    |     NEC2D5K7.EXE    5700       500\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}nec-pc-x.zip|     NEC2D8K1.EXE    8100      1006\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}             \\    NEC2D11K.EXE   11000      1852\par
\fs20{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
\f5\fs16{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}               (*) NEC2D.EXE should be in the directory from where you run\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}               the other executables, it is the only one that contains the\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}               DOS-extender. The others borrow it from NEC2D.EXE.\par
\f0\fs20{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}Nec2dXS engines are available for the following maximum number of segments:\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}Nec2dXS500\tab\tab max 500 segments\tab\tab (5 Mb)\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}Nec2dXS1k5\tab\tab max 1500 segments\tab\tab (40 Mb)\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}Nec2dXS3k0\tab\tab max 3000 segments\tab\tab (150 Mb)\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}Nec2dXS5k0\tab\tab max 5000 segments\tab\tab (512 Mb)\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}Nec2dXS8k0\tab\tab max 8000 segments\tab\tab (appr 1Gb)\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}Nec2dXS11k\tab\tab max 11000 segments\tab (appr 2Gb)\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}By default 4nec2 uses the smallest executable available for the number of segments specified in the *.nec inputfile. However when using the original nec2d engine this may cause problems on systems with 128 Mb of RAM or more. \par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}When using a 'simple' nec input file with few segments in combination with a 'small' exe and with a \lquote large\rquote  amount of RAM, a message "Program requires MS-DOS mode,...." might be displayed. If this is the case, increase the number of segments in the \lquote nr of segments\rquote  setting, till the message no longer appears. On the other hand, don't set a far too big number of segments, because this will decrease calculating performance.\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}For troubleshooting and additional information about engine selection, see: \ul troubleshooting\ulnone\v error\v0\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}ZIP files for the nec2d and the nec2dXS executables can be found at the \ldblquote Unofficial NEC archives\rdblquote : (See http://www.si-list.org/swindex2.html) or on the 4nec2 home-page at http://www.qsl.net/4nec2/index.html.\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab} These *.exe files should all be placed in your ..\\4nec2\\exe folder.\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}Additional notes:\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}(1) The Nec2d engine used is the one created by Jos Bergervoet.\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}(2) The Nec2dXS engine is based on the nec2dX engine made available by Raymond Anderson.\par
\pard{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlcont\pnf7\pnindent0{\pntxtb\'B7}}\nowidctlpar\ri-1800\qj (3) The Nec4D engines are available as a licensed product from Lawrence Livermore National Laboratory.\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
\pard{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlcont\pnf7\pnindent0{\pntxtb\'B7}}\nowidctlpar\ri-540\qj The Nec2dXS engine is an improved engine as it does not require the additional Somnec2d.exe program. Besides this, when running a Nec-2 frequency sweep the Real/SomNec ground data is recalculated for every frequency-Step. The original nec2d engine calculates this Real/SomNec ground data only once (using a negative conductivity setting. For more info, see nec-2 user-manual), so accuracy is improved. \par
\pard{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlcont\pnf7\pnindent0{\pntxtb\'B7}}\nowidctlpar\qj\super #$K\nosupersub\par
\pard{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlcont\pnf7\pnindent0{\pntxtb\'B7}}\nowidctlpar\ri-1800\qj\b\fs24 ItsHF\tab\tab Propagation prediction using the ItsHF package\par
\b0\fs20{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
\pard{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlcont\pnf7\pnindent0{\pntxtb\'B7}}\nowidctlpar\ri-432\qj In this paragraph the Area- and Point-to-Point Coverage options available with the Generate command are described in more detail. These both options are used in conjunction with the HFwin32 high frequency propagation-modeling program developed by NTIA/ITS, which is freely available on the Internet. (See http://elberts.its.bldrdoc.gov/hf.html) or do a Google search.\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}Within the combination of 4nec2 and the ItsHF package/program it is possible to create Area-Coverage maps or create Point-to-Point plots. By default the ItsHF package contains a number of pre-defined antenna types to be used for propagation prediction. 4nec2 however enables you to create your own ItsHF type antenna input files based on your (4)nec2 model. \par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
\pard{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlcont\pnf7\pnindent0{\pntxtb\'B7}}\nowidctlpar\qj On the Generate (F7) form you can choose between two 4nec2 specific types of ItsHF antenna files to create: \par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}- \uldb 360 degree Gain-table\ulnone\v type13\v0  (Type 13: 360 degree Gain-table (Gain table versus azimuth and & elevation angle)\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}- \uldb Gain @ 30 frequencies\ulnone\v type14\v0  (Type 14: Gain-table at 30 frequencies (Gain versus elevation angle & frequency)\par
\pard{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlcont\pnf7\pnindent0{\pntxtb\'B7}}\nowidctlpar\ri-432\qj\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}When running a propagation prediction both Transmitter (Xmtr) and Receiver (Rcvr) antenna must be specified. If <current> is set as the antenna (type) to use, the above selected antenna-type is automatically created  from your Nec model (if not already available) and used for the prediction. \par
\b{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}Running a prediction\par
\b0{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}Before running a propagation prediction you must choose between four types of plots:\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}\par
\pard{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnf7\pnindent360{\pntxtb\'B7}}\nowidctlpar\fi-375\li375\ri-630\qj\tx375 Map\tab\tab\tab Area-coverage plot showing propagation performance on a (partial) world-map.\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}Time\tab\tab Point-to-point chart showing propagation performance as function of time-of-day\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}Distance\tab Point-to-point chart showing propagation performance as function of distance\par
{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}Graph plot\tab Point-to-point chart showing propagation performance as function of time and frequency\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\ri-630\qj\par
After having selected the required plot-type additional settings can be specified and/or modified as listed below:\par
\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\fi-1140\li1140\ri-432\qj\ul Phi-max\ulnone\tab\par
\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\ri-432\qj (or Azim-max) When generating an ItsHF antenna-file you will have to specify the NEC based phi/azimuth angle for the main beam for the antenna under test. Usually this will be 0 or 90 degrees. This value is used so the NEC main-beam angle corresponds to the ItsHF main-beam azimuth angle of 0 degrees.\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\ri-630\qj\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\fi-1140\li1140\ri-432\qj\ul Bearing\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\ri-432\qj\par
\ulnone For a point-to-point type of plot (time, distance or graph) it is required to specify both the transmitter and receiver location. Both these locations determine the antenna direction (bearing) from transmitter to receiver and from receiver to transmitter. When changing one or both locations the antenna direction for the transmitter is automatically updated. Manual modification however is still possible. For an area-coverage plot a receiver location is not strictly needed. This location however is used to determine the default antenna direction.\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\ri-630\qj\par
\ul Map\par
\par
\ulnone This box let\rquote s you select between the different map \lquote sizes\rquote  for an Area-Coverage plot. Selection is disabled when a time, distance or graph type plot is selected. When using <default> the setting as specified in the 4nec2.ice (.rec or .voa) template file is used\ul\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\fi-1140\ri-630\qj\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\ri-630\qj Power\ulnone\tab\par
\par
Using this text-box you can overrule the default transmitting power as specified under '\uldb Settings\ulnone\v settings\v0  on the Main (F2) window.\ul  \par
\pard\nowidctlpar\fi-1140\ri-630\qj\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\ri-630\qj Param\ulnone\tab\par
\par
This selection box let\rquote s you select between the different parameters to visualize on the plot. Selection is disabled for a \lquote Graph\rquote  type of plot. For the moment you can select the following:\par
\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\ri-432 - <use default file>\tab\tab Use parameter(s) as specified in 4nec2 template file (see below).\line - Signal at receiver\tab\tab SDBW \endash  Monthly median signal power at receiver (dBW).\par
- Noise at receiver\tab\tab NDBW - Monthly median noise  power at receiver (dBW)\par
- Field strength\tab\tab\tab DBU \endash  Monthly median field strength at receive location (dBu)\par
- Signal/Noise ratio\tab\tab SNR \endash  Monthly median signal-to-noise ratio (dB)\par
- % above req. SNR\tab\tab REL - Time availability, % days/month SNR exceeds required SNR\par
- Max usable frequency\tab MUF - Maximum Usable Frequency \endash  monthly median (MHz)\par
- Radiation angle\tab\tab\tab ANGLE \endash  Radiation angle at Xmtr (degrees)\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\ri-630\qj\par
Note: Due to some strange inconsistancy between the ItsHF Time/Distance and Graph type of plots, with the Graph type plot it is required to manually select the required plot once the prediction calculations are done and the ItsHF plot-window pops-up. This manual intervention is not required for the Area-Coverage, Time and Distqance plots.\par
\par
\lquote Required SNR\rquote  is specified using the \lquote Mode of operations\rquote  available through the <More> button. \par
\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\fi-760\li760\ri-1800\qj See \uldb DBW and dBuV conversion\ulnone\v S-mtr\v0  for conversion from DBW and dBuV to S-meter readings.\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\ri-630\qj\par
\par
\ul Fixed\par
\ulnone\par
To set Fixed contour scaling for Area-coverage-maps and Graph-plots and fixed Y-axis scaling for time- and distance-plots. When not set scaling is done automatically by the ItsHF program. Fixed Contour/Y-axis scaling is usefull when using the picture-viewer to scroll through a set of plot-files. Sets of plot-files are created when a none-zero time-span is selected for area-coverage map\rquote s or when the 4nec2 \uldb Variable-sweeper\ulnone\v\f3 Eval\v0\f0  is used.\par
\par
You can use the ItsUtil.exe program (\lquote Run -> ItsHF colors and scaling\rquote  on \lquote Main [F2]\rquote  ) to change Color-, Contour- and/or Y-axis-scaling for Area-coverage, Graph and Time/Distance plots to meet specific needs. \par
\par
\ul Month\ulnone\tab\par
\par
To select the (starting) month of interest. Furthermore this sets the so-called Smoothed Sunspot Number, (SSN) which is an important propagation parameter. When selecting <def>, the value from the template file is used. If a certain month is selected, this value together with the specified year is used to let 4nec2 automatically get the proper SSN from the internal \lquote sunspot.txt\rquote  file. \ul\par
\ulnone\par
\ul SSN\ulnone\tab\par
\par
To select the year for setting the Smoothed Sunspot Number (see above) this selection box is used. To manually specify the SSN value (in the second text box), select the \lquote manual\rquote  setting.\ul\par
\ulnone\par
\ul UTC\ulnone\tab\par
\par
The UTC selection box is used to specify the (starting) time-of-day for which a (set of) prediction(s) should be done. Set <def> to use the value from the template file.\par
\ul\par
Span\ulnone\tab\par
\par
You can run multiple Area-Coverage or Point-to-Point Time, Distance or Graph plots for a selected time-span. \par
When running an Area-Coverage predictions for a certain time-span, multiple plots are created. All plots are automatically cut and pasted into the new \lquote\uldb picture-viewer\ulnone\v IDH_capt\v0\rquote  window with which you can manually scroll through or switch between the different plots.\par
\par
When running a Point-to-Point Distance prediction for a certain time-span, you must use the \lquote New plot\rquote  menu-bar option on the plot-window to manually select between the different plots. This same option is used to select between the different frequencies as specified using the <More> button (see below). Time, Distance and Graph plots are not automatically copied to the \lquote picture-viewer\rquote  window. \par
\par
When an Area-Coverage map is selected additional \lquote frequency\rquote  and \lquote month\rquote  span-selections are available. These are used to create a set of plots for each frequency as specified using the <More> button or a set of plots for 6 months with 1 or 2 month interval.\par
\par
\ul Location\ulnone\tab\par
\par
Both of these command buttons are used to specify the transmitter (Xmtr) or Receiver (Rcvr) location. A new window will pop-up, in which you first will have to select the file-name containing a list of locations to choose from. After this selection all locations in the file are listed on the second part of the window. You can click the top-row for a specific column of the list to sort the list. You can select a certain location by double clicking in the desired location. \ul\par
\ulnone\par
\ul Antenna\ulnone\tab\par
\par
Using these both command buttons you can specify the antenna used at the transmitter (Xmtr) or Receiver (Rcvr) location. A new window will pop-up, in which you first will have to select the folder containing the antenna files to choose from. \par
The 4nec2 folder contains the type-13 and -14 antenna-files created in previous 4nec2 sessions. Select <Current> to automatically create (or re-use) the type-13 or type-14 antenna-file for the current antenna under test.\par
After selection all antennas contained in the selected folder are listed on the second part of the window. You can click the top-row for a specific column of the list to sort the list. You can select a certain antenna by double clicking in the desired antenna. \ul\par
\ulnone\par
The \lquote Transmit\rquote  and \lquote  Receive\rquote  folders contain general HAM related transmit- and receiving antennas. All receiving antennas are omni-directional and the Dipole- and Yagi-types are artificial derivations of their directional counterparts. \par
\par
These omni-directional receiving antennas are especially usefull for Area-coverage prediction, because for each receiver in the \lquote grid of receivers\rquote  and as seen from the receiver another direction/bearing applies. If you would use a directional receiving-antenna with a direction/bearing of 0 degrees (aimed North) the receivers North of the transmitter would be pointing with their backs towards the transmitter whereas the receivers to the South would be properly aimed. If propagation would be the same in all directions and you would use an omnidirectional transmitting antenna this would result in unequal coverage. \par
\par
These artificial receiving antennas are also perfectly usable as a receiving antenna in point-to-point predictions. \par
\par
\ul More (button)\ulnone\tab\par
\par
This button is used to specify additional circuit related parameters:\par
\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\li380\ri-630\qj\ul Mode of operation\ulnone  which determines the minimum required signal to noise ratio for the selected mode. (Used to calculate the \lquote % above req. SNR\rquote  (REL) parameter)\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\ri-630\qj\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\li380\ri-630\qj\lquote\ul Noise level at receiver\ulnone\rquote , which specifies the Man-made noise level at the receiver location(s). \ul\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\ri-630\qj\ulnone\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\li380\ri-432\qj Above settings are used when running a \lquote Signal/Noise ratio\rquote  (SNR) or \lquote % above req. SNR\rquote  (REL) prediction\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\li375\ri-630\qj\par
\lquote\ul Extra Frequencies\rquote\ulnone , for the Point-to-Point Time- and Distance-plots, additional frequencies to calculate a plot for can be specified. After prediction calculations are done you can use the \lquote New plot\rquote  menu-bar option on the ItsHF time- or distance plot-windows to select a plot for a different frequency or even for all frequencies at once.\ul\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\ri-630\qj\ulnone\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\li375\ri-432\qj Using the \lquote HAM-bands\rquote  or \lquote  Broadcast\rquote  buttons, initial/default frequencies for Ham- or Broadcast bands are set. One can manually change these values.\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\ri-432\qj  \par
\par
\par
When using the ItsHF package its important to keep in mind that the ItsHF programs uses azimuth and elevation angles whereas 4nec2 and NEC by default use Phi and Theta angles. For later 4nec2 versions however it is possible to switch between Phi/Theta and Azimuth/Elevation presentation.\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\ri-630\qj\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\ri-432\qj When elevation increases from 0 to 90 degrees, the theta angle changes from 90 or -90 degrees to 0 degrees. Azimuth increases in a clockwise direction from 0 to 360 degrees. Phi increases in an anti-clockwise direction from 0 to 360 degrees.\par
\par
All automatically generated area-coverage or point-to-point input-files are derived from default 4nec2.ice, 4nec2.rec or 4nec2.voa template files as located in your \lquote ..\\ItsHF\\areadata\\default\rquote  or \lquote ..\\ItsHF\\saved\\default\rquote  folders. This is especially true if, for the selection boxes on the Generate form, the <default> selection is used. When other selections are used the settings as specified in the template files are overruled by the setting as selected. \par
You are free to modify these template files. For instance to set a larger area-coverage grid-density. You can do so by using \lquote Run -> ItsHF Area Coverage\rquote  in the \lquote Main (F2)\rquote   window, select the \lquote default folder and open the 4nec2.* file. After modification, save this file under the same name. Next time you run a predication the new settings are used (if not overruled by the user-settings on the Generate form). \par
 \par
\pard\nowidctlpar\ri-630\qj To use the ItsHF software package it must be installed on your computer system. Use '\uldb Settings\ulnone\v settings\v0  -> Folders' to specify your ItsHF home folder. In this same\v settings\v0  Settings-menu you can choose between the ICEPAC, VOACAP or REC533 model to use for the propagation-prediction. At the time of writing this the VOACAP and ICEPAC models are considered to be the most accurate. Note that folder-names containing more than eight characters are not allowed. If possible use the default C:\\ITSHFBC folder.\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\ri-432\qj\par
\par
\ul Propagation Prediction using the Variable-sweeper\par
\ulnone\par
You may use the \uldb Variable-sweeper\ulnone\v Eval\v0  to see the effect of changing certain antenna dimensions on propagation performance. To do so, first select \lquote  Sweep\rquote  as the required function and then select \lquote type-13\rquote  or \lquote type-14\rquote  as the antenna-type of interest using the \lquote Option\rquote  selection-box on the optimizer/sweeper form. During sweep a number of ItsHF antenna-, input- and output/plot-files are created. For this, the 4nec2 (antenna) filename is truncated to six characters and a two character sequence-number is added to this left-over filename to distinguish between the different calculation steps. This may produce a lot of files in the ..\\antenna\\4nec2, ..\\areadata\\4nec2 or ..\\saved\\4nec2 folders. If disk-space is an issue you may delete these files without further problems. \par
\par
During the sweep all created plot-files are cut and pasted into the \lquote\uldb picture-viewer\ulnone\v IDH_capt\v0\rquote  window with which you can manually scroll through the different images if calculations and plottings are finished. You can use the \lquote Save\rquote  or \lquote Print\rquote  buttons to save or print specific images. Use the \lquote Sel(ect)\rquote  button to select part of an image for save or print.\par
\par
\par
\ul Steps to distinguish when running a prediction:\par
\ulnone\par
For Area-coverage map generation 4nec2 processes the following steps:\par
\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\fi-360\li360\ri-432\qj\tx360 1)\tab Nec is run and depending on the antenna-type selected, output data for a 90 * 360 sample far-field request or for a 90 sample far-field request at 30 frequencies is generated and translated into a type-13 or type-14 antenna input-file with name <name>.n13 or <name>.n14.\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\fi-360\li360\ri-432\qj 2)\tab From the ItsHF parameters specified on the Generate form and (depending on the model selected in the settings menu) a <name>.ice, <name>.rec or <name>.voa Area-Coverage input-file is created.   \par
\pard\nowidctlpar\fi-360\li360\ri-432\qj\tx360 3)\tab Using both the Area-coverage and Antenna input-files the ItsHF engine for the specified model is run and an Area-coverage plot/output-file is created with name <name>.ig1, <name>.rg1 or <name>.vg1\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\fi-360\li360\ri-432\qj 4)\tab If not specified otherwise in the settings menu the generated output/plot-file is displayed on the screen. You can move the mouse around the Map and click a location to read distance and parameter values or use the \lquote Parameter\rquote  menu-option to select another parameter to plot. \par
\pard\nowidctlpar\ri-432\qj\par
For Point-to-point plots, 4nec2 processes the following steps:\par
\par
\pard{\pntext\f0 1)\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pnf0\pnindent360\pnstart1\pndec{\pntxta)}}
\nowidctlpar\fi-360\li360\ri-432\qj\tx360 Nec is run and depending on the antenna-type selected, output data for a 90 * 360 sample far-field request or for a 90 sample far-field request at 30 frequencies is generated and translated into a type-13 or type-14 antenna input-file with name <name>.n13 or <name>.n14.\par
{\pntext\f0 2)\tab}From the ItsHF parameters specified on the Generate form and depending on the model selected in the Settings menu a <name>.ice, <name>.rec or <name>.voa Point-to-point input-file is created   \par
\pard\nowidctlpar\fi-360\li360\ri-432\qj\tx360 3)\tab Using both the Point-to-Point and Antenna input-files the ItsHF engine for the specified model is run and an Time-, Distance or Graph- output-file is created with name <model>T.dat, <model>D.dat or <model>G.dat\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\fi-360\li360\ri-432\qj 4)\tab If not specified otherwise in the Settings menu the generated output-data is displayed as a Time, Distance or Graph plot on the screen. You can manually select between the different time- and/or frequency plots using the \lquote New plot\rquote  menu-option.\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\ri-432\qj\par
\par
The following files/extensions are used in the process:\par
\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\fi380\li1900\ri-432\qj ICEPAC\tab\tab\tab VOACAP \tab\tab\tab REC533\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\ri-432\qj - Type 13 ant-file:\tab\tab <name>.n13\tab\tab\tab <name>.n13\tab\tab\tab <name>.n13\par
- Type 14 ant-file:\tab\tab <name>.n14\tab\tab\tab <name>.n14\tab\tab\tab <name>.n14\par
- Template-file:\tab\tab\tab 4nec2.ice\tab\tab\tab 4nec2.voa\tab\tab\tab 4nec2.rec\par
- Area Input-file:\tab\tab\tab <name>.ice\tab\tab\tab <name>.voa\tab\tab\tab <name>.rec\par
- P-to-P Input-file:\tab\tab <name>.ice\tab\tab\tab <name>.voa\tab\tab\tab <name>.rec\par
- Area output-file:\tab\tab\tab <name>.ig1\tab\tab\tab <name>.vg1\tab\tab\tab <name>.rg1\par
- P-to-P output-file\tab\tab icepacX.dat\tab\tab\tab voacapX.dat\tab\tab\tab rec533X.dat\par
\par
For the P-to-P output file the \lquote X\rquote  character is replaced by T, D or G for Time, Distance and Graph plot requests.\par
\par
Type-13 and \endash 14 antenna input-files are created in folder \lquote ..\\ItsHF\\antennas\\4nec2\rquote .  \par
Area-coverage input-files are created in folder \lquote ..\\ItsHF\\areadata\\4nec2\rquote .\par
Area-coverage plot-files are created in folder \lquote ..\\ItsHF\\areadata\\4nec2\rquote .  \par
Point-to-point input-files are created in folder \lquote ..\\ItsHF\\saved\\4nec2\rquote . \par
Point-to-point output-files are created in folder \lquote ..\\ItsHF\\run\rquote .  \par
\par
The filename <name> for your antenna under test is truncated to eight characters for use with the ItsHF software.\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\qj\ul\page\ulnone\super #$K\nosupersub\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\ri-1800\qj\b\fs24 360 degree Gain-table\par
\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\ri-432\qj\b0\fs20 (Type 13: 360 degree Gain-table [Gain table versus azimuth and & elevation angle])\par
\par
When using the ItsHF software to run a propagation prediction, one needs to specify the antenna-types used at the transmitter (Xmtr) and receiver (Rcvr) locations. ItsHF by itself already contains a large number of more or less general antenna-types. Performance (radiation pattern) for these pre-defined antenna types is determined by equations, which are run by the ItsHF engine when a prediction is done. \par
You can view the far-field radiation patterrn for these predefined antennas by using the ItsHF built-in antenna-viewer. This viewer is started using the \lquote Run -> ItsHF antenna-view\rquote  menu-command on the \lquote Main (F2)\rquote  window.\par
\par
On the other side ItsHF enables us to use so called gain-tables, in which for each theta/phi value covering half the hemisfere a gain-value is listed. When propagation in a certain direction has to be calculated, ItsHF consults these tables and uses the listed gain for the required direction.   \par
\par
When using 4nec2 together with the ItsHF software we distinguish two different antenna-types using gain-\par
Tables: a) the type-13 antenna, containing a full 3D 90*360 degree gain-table for a single frequency and b) the type-14 antenna, containing a set of 1*90 degree gain-tables for specified phi/azimuth-angle, but for all integer frequencies from 1 to 30 Mhz.\par
\par
Both of these antenna-types are created using 4nec2. This is automatically done by selecting the required antenna-type in the \lquote Generate (F7)\rquote  window. If an antenna-file for the selected type is already available it is re-used. You can overrule this behavour by checking the \lquote run Nec\rquote  check-box.\par
\par
The \lquote 360 degree Gain-table\rquote  option on the Generate window lets you generate 360 * 90 degree Gain-table file for a single frequency (type-13). This type of file is the default antenna file to use for calculating and plotting Area-coverage Maps for your antenna structure under test. \par
\par
When running a Time- or Distance plot for the working/design frequency for the antenna under test (as is the case running a plot through 4nec2) a type-13 antenna files is the one to use. However when Time/Distance plots for multiple frequencies are run, \uldb type-14\ulnone\v type14\v0  files are preferred. \par
\par
When using a type-13 file to run a Area-coverage map for multiple frequencies or to create a Point-to-point Graph type of plot (propagation versus time and frequency) keep in mind that this single 360 degree radiation pattern is used for all frequencies steps ranging from 2 to 30 Mhz, so type-14 antenna-files (Gain @30 frequencies) are the better choice.\par
\par
\par
\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\qj\page\super #$K\nosupersub\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\ri-1800\qj\b\fs24 Gain @ 30 frequencies\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\ri-432\qj\b0\fs20\par
(Type 14: Gain-table at 30 frequencies [Gain table versus elevation angle & frequency])\par
\par
In the description about the \uldb type-13\ulnone\v type13\v0  antenna-file it is explained how these antenna-files are created and how these are used by 4nec2/ItsHF.\par
\par
The \lquote Gain @ 30 frequencies\rquote  option on the Generate window lets you create a set of 1 * 90 degree gain tables for a single Phi/Azimuth angle. For each whole/integer frequency between 1 and 30 Mhz a set of 90 gain values (one for each theta/elevation angle) is generated.\par
\par
The Phi/azimuth angle for which each vertical pattern is generated is defined by the difference between the real \lquote bearing\rquote  between the Transmitter- and Receiver location (listed behind the Xmtr/Rcvr antenna button) and the \lquote Bearing\rquote  (antenna direction) value as (manually) specified on the \lquote Generate\rquote  form. \par
\par
This type of file (type-14) is the default antenna input-file for calculating and plotting the point-to-point Graph type of plots (frequency/time). These graphs for instance can show you the MUF, SNR or field strength as function of frequency and time of day. \par
\par
Type-14 files can also be used for multi-frequency Area-coverage and point-to-point Time- and Distance-plots. When a none integer (design) frequency is specified used/selected the pattern to be used for the prediction is interpolated from the patterns for the two nearby integer frequencies. \par
\par
When generating a type-14 antenna file through 4nec2 note however that segmentation can become an issue. because both at 2 and at 30 Mhz Nec-requirements must be met. Depending on the frequency range of interest and the accuracy needed, you can run a default 4nec2 far-fiels 3D pattern at both the upper- and lower frequencies to check if Nec-requirements are not violated or not, and if so, how much.\par
\par
On the other hand however one will rarely use a single antenna for all frequencies between 2 and 30 Mhz. For those who would like to specify different antennas for specific frequency-bands or \endash ranges one could use the original ItsHF point-to-point user inteface. With this it is possible to specify up to 10 different antennas with corresponding frequency-ranges.\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\ri-360\qj\f3\par
\page\f0\par
\super #$K\nosupersub\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\ri-1800\qj\b\fs28 3D viewer form\tab\tab F9\tab Show geometry/pattern in real-time 3D.\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\ri-360\qj\b0\fs20\par
The 3D-viewer form is available only for the extended freeware version 4nec2X, and requires a computer system on which the Microsoft DirectX 8.0 or later hardware\endash acceleration drivers are installed. For further information about how to check for DirectX or how to install, see the _ReadMeFirst.txt file.\par
\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\ri-432\qj The 3D viewer is used to display structure geometry and/or 3D far- and near-field pattern in real-time 3D. Compared with the Geometry-view F3, this viewer shows the structure much more as it is in real-life. It also shows the real wire-radius. \par
However when the radius becomes too small to be visible, for instance for wire-antennas, a correction is applied on the wire-radius, to keep it visible. A check box is available on the right of the form, which is enabled if it\rquote s possible to re-enable the real-radius display. \par
This way it is very easy to see if wires are overlaid or intersect (at unexpected places) or not. Also at junctions with short fat segments the intersected area should not be more than about 1/3 of the segment length. With some experience it\rquote s also possible, for wire grids, to check the \lquote equal area\rquote  rule.  \par
\pard\nowidctlpar\ri-360\qj\par
When patches are included in the structure geometry, the \lquote shiny\rquote  side of the patch is the one from where the normal-vector is pointing outwards. This side of the patch should be considered as being part of the NEC calculation process.\par
\par
Use the structure drop-down box to select different structure-display options.\par
Use the \lquote Res\rquote  button to reset all zoom-, shift- and/or rotate-actions.\par
\par
The following key\rquote s and mouse-buttons are available to change the viewing-position.\par
\par
\tab\tab\tab\tab\tab\ul Key-combination:\ulnone\tab\tab\tab\ul Mouse-action:\par
\ulnone\par
- Rotate structure\tab Left/Right or Up/Down arrow\tab Move mouse with left button pressed\par
- Move structure\tab\tab Ctrl+Left/Right-arrow\tab\tab\tab Move mouse with right button pressed\par
- Forward/backward\tab Ctrl+Page-up/down\tab\tab\tab Move mouse with both button pressed\par
- Zoom-in/out\tab\tab Page-up/down\par
\par
When the display of currents is enabled, and also a freq-sweep calculation was performed, the function of the cursor/arrow key\rquote s is changed from structure movement/rotation to frequency \lquote movement\rquote .\~\par
\par
The upper drop-down box lets you select different types of structure (geometry) display. If \lquote wires\rquote  or \lquote segments\rquote  is selected, the red end is end-1 and the green end is end-2. \par
If \lquote currents\rquote  is enabled use the \lquote Log factor\rquote  scroll-bar to visualize small currents on gridded/external objects.\par
\par
If NEC output-data is available the drop-down boxes on the right of the form are enabled. This lets you visualize the 3D far- or near field E/H pattern in 3D. You have the choise between single-color or multi-color mode. In multi-color mode a blue to red scale is applied to the pattern for which the color corresponds to the color-scale on the left of the form.\par
\par
When, for the far-field, the 2D-slice display option is selected, a 2D-pattern slice is displayed for a certain theta/elevation or phi/azimuth value. You can change the Theta/Elevation or Phi/Azimuth angle for which the slice is displayed, on the \lquote Pattern (F4)\rquote  form while simultaneously watching the 3D form. In the \lquote Pattern (F4)\rquote  form, use the space-bar to switch between horizontal/vertical slice.\par
\par
When on the \uldb Pattern\ulnone\v IDH_F4\v0 -form multiple far-field patterns are selected to be compared against each other, you can also switch between these patterns on the 3D-viewer form. Use the selection-box in which the file-name is displayed to select one of the patterns. You may also use the \lquote <\rquote  and \lquote >\rquote  keys (with or without <Shift>)\par
\par
On certain computer monitors it showed up that color display sometimes is not optimal. To overcome this \lquote problem\rquote , use Ctrl+C to bring-up the color settings window. Select the appropriate structure part and use the sliders to change color or to alter background- or, directional-light intensity.\par
\par
When no key\rquote s are pressed for a certain amount af time, the structure is rotated automatically. You may maximize the window and use this functionallity as a kind of screen-saver. You may use the \lquote Z\rquote  key to start to spinning directly without waiting for the time-out.\par
\par
When structures are viewed with a large number of (bent)segments and/or junctions, a certain simplification is done when creating the 3D picture. For instance, by default, all wire ends are closed and wire-radius-changes and junctions for more than two segments are smoothened.\par
\par
For very high-segment structures these actions are not performed. This is represented by the \lquote Quality\rquote  slide bar on the lower right corner. Eventually one can use this slide-bar to change the default 3D structure quality and increase speed, ranging in three steps from left to right. \par
\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\fi-360\li720\ri-1800\qj\tx720 -\tab Left\tab\tab :\tab Show \lquote simple\rquote  structure as a wire-grid.\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\fi-360\li720\ri-1800\qj -\tab Middle\tab :\tab Show structure with open ends and none smoothed junctions\par
-\tab Right\tab :\tab Show high-res structure with closed ends and smoothed junctions\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\ri-1800\qj\par
FPS\tab = number of frames (screen refreshes) per second.\par
Tri\rquote s\tab\tab = number of triangles used to constuct the 3D objects\par
\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\ri-180\qj When a large number of far/near-field points is requested or when structures with a large number of segments and with \lquote high-quality\rquote  are viewed, it could be necessarry to increase the \lquote Max-nr of 3D-points\rquote  in the \uldb setting\ulnone\v settings\v0 -menu. If so, a message will inform you about this possibility. However, because this setting also affects the amount of video-card memory used, large values can sometimes result in strange video effects. If so, lower this setting.\par
