\b\f0\fs28 Matching\tab\tab\tab F10\tab Impedance matching networks.\par
\b0\fs20\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\qj For maximum power transfer from the source to your antenna/load and/or to reduce transmission-line losses it is needed to match your antenna to the characteristic impedance (Z0) of your feed/transmission-line. In many cases however it is not possible to choose all impedance such that overall matched conditions result. These situations require that matching networks be used. \par
\par
In general, matching networks are constructed with reactive components only so that minimal loss is added to the overall network. For frequencies up to approximately 500 MHz matching networks containing lumped elements (L, Pi or T networks) may be used. The circuit elements (capacitors and inductors) must be small enough relative to wavelength.\par
\par
Another way of antenna to feedline matching is called Stub-Matching and uses parallel or series stubs. These stubs are open or closed (at the end) pieces of transmission-line with different impedance placed somewhere along the feedline. \par
A third method is called Series-Matching. This method uses a series matching system consisting of three sections of transmission line. The middle section does have different characteristic impedance.\par
\par
\f3\par
\pard\keepn\nowidctlpar\s2\qj\b\f0 L, Pi or T-network matching\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\qj\b0\par
Using L, Pi or T-networks it is theoretically possible to match any source/load impedance combination. However in practice certain reactance/resistance values could result in additional component losses and the use of lumped elements does reduce your overall system bandwidth.\par
\par
4nec2 supports the automatic calculation of the required components for low- and high-pass L, Pi and T(ee) networks using the \lquote Matching (F10)\rquote  window/form.\par
\par
When this form is first displayed 4nec2 checks if already a matching network is present. If not, the antenna/structure input-impedance value and the design-frequency is copied into the \lquote Matching (F10)\rquote  form and the Z0 as specified under the \lquote Settings\rquote  menu is set as the (source) impedance to match. With these impedance\rquote s then the required component-values to create low- and high-pass L, Pi and Tee networks are calculated and displayed on the screen. \par
\par
For both inductors and capacitors their quality \lquote Q\rquote  may be specified using the Q-coil and Q-cap. Text-boxes. By default capacitors are considered lossles (indefinitely high Q, entered as zero), whereas inductors are given a default Q of 250.  If sufficiently high (100-300) then the low series resistance will not influence the required network components for a 1:1 match much.\par
\par
To let 4nec2 automatically add the matching network to your (4)nec2 antenna design, first select the required network and then click the \ldblquote use network\rdblquote  button and let 4nec2 generate new NEC data. You can use the default option \ldblquote use original file\rdblquote  to generate only the new input-impedance.\par
\par
If done so, 4nec2 will create a (far-away) dummy wire, place the voltage-source on it and connect this dummy wire with the original excitation wire/segment through a network NT-card. The Y11, Y12 and Y22 values to be used with the NT card are calculated when the network is selected. After generation the new input impedance together with the resulting losses for the selected matching network will be displayed on the \lquote Main (F2)\rquote  window.\par
\par
As long as the \lquote Matching (F10)\rquote  window is active you can re-generate using other selected networks. Once the \lquote Matching\rquote  window is closed you will first have to generate the \lquote raw\rquote  input-impedance using the default \lquote Generate (F7)\rquote  function, before you are able to use the \lquote Matching (F10)\rquote  window again.\par
\par
For Pi and Tee networks by default 4nec2 uses the lowest possible Q. This to minimize losses. If more manageable components or extra attenuation of undesired frequencies is desired (for Pi and Tee) a different/higher value may be specified in the min-network-Q textbox. A higher network-Q however will increase network-losses and reduce bandwidth. \par
\par
\ul Note:\par
\ulnone\par
- You may manually change Z-ant, Z-src and/or Frequency to let 4nec2 directly reflect these changes in the calculated L, Pi or Tee network-values. This way you can use this form as a general tool to match for other impedances and/or frequencies\par
- After a certain L, Pi or Tee network has been selected, you can also manually change component values (L, C, Ql or Qc) or the network-Q to see how these changes directly affect the NT network parameters Y11 to Y22.\par
- Click the \lquote use network\rquote  button to see the effect on overall input-impedance and power losses in the network due to your manual changes (tolerances) of Network component values. \par
\par
You will see that using inferior components and thus lowering component-Q (and increasing network-Q for Pi/Tee) will increase losses and deviate the input-impedance more and more from the specified Z0. On the other hand an infinite high coil- and capacitor-Q (enter a value of zero) will deliver an exact 1:1 match.\par
\par
Click the <Reset> button to restore all fields to their initial values. \par
\par
\par
\pard\keepn\nowidctlpar\s2\qj\b Stub/series matching\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\qj\b0\par
You can reach the stub/series matching form by clicking the \lquote stub\rquote  button on the upper-right on the Network matching form.\par
\par
When the form is first displayed 4nec2 checks if a matching network is already included. If not, the antenna/structure input-impedance value is copied onto the Stub-matching form as \lquote Z-load (antenna). The design-frequency and the Z0 (as specified on the Main-form\rquote s settings-menu) are also copied onto this form.\par
\par
With the \lquote select matching network\rquote  selection box you can choose between the matching network to use. For now, below types are available:\par
\par
- \ul Series section\ulnone ; this type of match uses three sections of transmission line in series, in which the middle transmission line does have different impedance. The impedance transformation is defined by the Z0 of the series section, the length of (default) feed/transmission-line from antenna to the middle series-section and the length of this series-section. An arbitrary third (lower) length of (default) feed-line connects the series section to your rig.\par
\par
- \ul Parallel stub\ulnone , open or short; this type of matching-network also uses a second different type of transmission-line which is connected somewhere between the antenna and your rig on the feedline. The parallel stub is connected as the name already implies in parallel with your feedline. The impedance transformation is defined by the distance between antenna and the point on the feedline where the stub is connected and the characteristic impedance and length of the stub-line. The required lengths are influenced by the fact weather you choose to use a short or open end for you stub.\par
\par
- \ul Series stub\ulnone , open or short;  this matching-network uses the same principles as the parallel stub, but now the stub is connected in series with one of both feeder-lines. For the series stub one can also choose between an open or a short stub.\par
 \par
Also included on the form are two selection boxes to select both types of feed/transmission-line. The first sets your default type of feedline; the second sets the characteristics for the series-section or stub-line. When \lquote user-specified\rquote  is selected you can manually enter characteristic-impedance (Z0) and/or velocity-factor (Vf) values.\par
\par
For each selection the resulting line-length results are displayed in the lower \lquote edit/select results\rquote  portion of the window.  If \lquote none\rquote  is displayed, there is no valid solution for the selected network and/or type of transmission-line(s). You will have to select another network or transmission-line.\par
For each selection 2 possible solutions are available, labeled A and B. When stub-matching is used one can furthermore select between using a short or open stub. This selection is done by using the \lquote select solution\rquote  selection-box or by clicking on one of the length text-boxes. Values highlighted represent the actual selection.\par
\par
To use the selected matching-network with specified lengths in your model, click the \lquote use selection\rquote  button. 4nec2 will now automatically add the required transmission lines to your model with the specified lengths and configuration and calculate the results, which are displayed on the Main(F2) and Geometry(F3) form.\par
\par
You can also manually overwrite the lengths as initially calculated by 4nec2 on the stub-matching form. This way you are able to see what results you get if you do not exactly follow the calculated line-lengths.\par
\f3\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\ri-1800\qj\f0\page\super #$K\nosupersub\par
\b\fs28 Smith Chart\tab\tab F11\tab\tab Show impedance and SWR on Smith-chart\par
\b0\fs20\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\ri-432\qj In this form the input impedance is plotted on a Smith-chart. When a Nec based frequency-sweep was done more than one impedance point is plotted. The active SWR is displayed as a circle. On the left of the Form additional information such as reflection loss, SWR in dB, refl-coeff (U/I), refl-coeff (P) and return loss is displayed. \par
\par
With the Shit+left or Shift+Right buttons you can specify any length of transmission line connected between the load and the generator and read the resulting impedance. With Ctrl+P you can capture and \ul save/print\ulnone\v IDH_capt\v0  the Smith-chart form for further graphical analysis.\par
\par
When you requested a frequency-sweep you can 'walk' through each frequency with the RIGHT and LEFT arrow-keys. The corresponding frequency and the real and imaginary values for the impedance are displayed in the lower right of the form. With the 'X' button you can switch between polar- or cartesian-notation. With the 'Home' button you can switch between a normalized chart or a chart based on the characteristic impedance.\par
\par
With pressing the Right or Left button together with the SHIFT button a second phase line is displayed. With this line you may specify a length of feed-line. On the lower left of the form a second pair of values is displayed, showing the real and imaginary values for the impedance on the other side of this feed-line, corresponding with the given SWR and phase angle.\par
\par
When selecting a phase difference of 180 degrees (lambda/4) this can also be used for reading the admittance values for the selected impedance. You can add and remove your own user impedance/admittance points by pressing the Insert and Delete keys.\par
\par
The grid for the Smith chart can be changed between fine and course with the corresponding option under the Show menu-bar option. For those who want to further customize their grid layout; the layout is described in the Smith.dat files in the \ldblquote\\exe\rdblquote  folder, but before changing it, be sure you made a backup or this file.\par
\par
Position your mouse and push <Insert> to add manual points, or use right mouse-button. Push <Delete> to remove last manual point. \par
\par
Use the \lquote Export\rquote  menu-bar option to export your smith-chart data in Touchstone (Z or S params) or Microsmith (*.gam) format. \par
Use the \lquote Import\rquote  menu-bar option to (re)import a Touchstone (Z or S prams) or Microsmith (*.gam) data/export-file. When importing data you are asked if existing data-points should be cleared ot not. If existing data is cleared, the new import data is also available through the line-chart form.\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\ri-1800\qj\b\page\super #$K\nosupersub\b0\par
\b\fs28 The Optimizer and sweeper\tab\tab F12\par
\fs20\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\ri-450\qj\b0 The optimizer/sweeper is started by pressing the F12 key or by selecting calculate -> optimize on the menu-bar in the Main window. The optimizer, as part of the 5.4 and later versions, contains four different options:\par
\par
- Optimize\tab Use the traditional hill-climbing method to optimize you design\par
- Evolve\tab\tab Use Genetic Algorithms to evolve (optimize) your antenna design  \par
- Sweep\tab\tab Use the Sweeper to visualize the effect of user specified design/variable changes.\par
- Conv-test\tab To validate your model by running an automatic Convergence-test\par
\par
You may select between one of these options with the "Function" box on the upper-left of the optimizer window. With the "Option" box you can select between different sub-options. For most cases 'Default' will be the appropriate choise.\par
\par
The traditional hill-climbing optimizer was based on principles as described by Brian Beezley and used by the AO (antenna-optimizer) program. A scaled down freeware version of this program is available at the ARRL web-site.\par
\par
For a description of how to \lquote evolve\rquote  your desing using the genetic algorithm based optimizer see \uldb Genetic Optimizer\ulnone\v GAopt\v0 , but before doing so, please read the below section about how to specify the antenna properties and variables to optimize/evolve. \par
A description of the traditional hill-climbing optimizer is given below, a description of the  \uldb Sweeper\ulnone\v Eval\v0  function is given in a separate section. How to run a convergence-test is described in \uldb Conv-test\ulnone\v Conv\v0 .\par
\par
When you want to use the optimizer the 4nec2 input file must include variables (constants). These variables serve as input for the optimizer. You may specify any combination of variables (and/or equations) anywhere in your 4nec2 input file (GW, LD, EX, FR card).\par
You can select only \ldblquote simple\rdblquote  variables (constants) as the ones to optimize. These are variables not containing any expressions like +, -, *, functions and/or other equations. These "simple" variables are the ones that are altered during the optimization process. (See the 3el-inverted-V.nec file for a more dedicated example).\par
\par
The optimizer window is devided into four different sections, one input and three output sections.\par
\par
\ul Antenna properties to optimize\par
\ulnone\par
The main input-section is named 'settings', and is used to specify the variable(s) to modify and the (antenna) properties to optimize. These properties are SWR (standing wave ratio) ,Gain (forward gain or Electric-fld), F/B (Front to back ration), F/R (Front to Rear ratio), R-a (Input resistance), X-a (input reactance) and/or Eff. (Efficiency). When the 'include surface wave' box is checked, the Gain property defaults to E-fld.\par
\par
For each of these properties you must specify the importance of it by specifying a value between 0 and 100% (weighting factor). This factor determines the effect on the overall performance for the corresponding property. For instance when optimizing only on SWR, the SWR factor is 100% and the other factors are set to zero. You can toggle this weighting factor between 0 and 100 by right clicking on the "SWR", "Gain", "F/B", "F/R", "R-a", "X-a" or "Eff." text label or on the corresponding text box. Other values have to be entered by keyboard. (Negative values or values > 100% are allowed).\par
\par
By default the SWR and X-in properties are minimized and the Gain, F/B, F/R, R-in and Eff. properties are maximized.  In order to change these defaults or to specify your own targer value, click with the right mouse-button on the corresponding text-label (SWR to Eff). A pop-up menu is displayed in which you can specify if you want to maximize or minimize this property or if you want it to reach a user-specified target value.\par
\par
The following targets are set when optimization towards minimum or maximum (the default) is selected:\par
\par
Property\tab\tab\tab Min\tab\tab Max\tab\par
\par
SWR\tab\tab\tab 1\tab\tab 100\par
Gain\tab\tab\tab -999\tab 100\tab\par
F/B\tab\tab\tab\tab -999\tab +999\par
F/R\tab\tab\tab\tab -999\tab +999\par
R-a\tab\tab\tab\tab 0\tab\tab 9999\tab\tab\par
X-a\tab\tab\tab\tab 0\tab\tab +9999\par
Eff.\tab\tab\tab\tab 0\tab\tab 100\par
\par
When no (wire)loading cards (LD) are included, optimizing on efficiency has no effect.\par
\par
User specified targets are accepted for values anywhere between -9999 and +9999. Note however that if one specifies a target of +9999 for, for instance, forward Gain, this does not improve optimizer accuracy. A target in or near the range of theoretical (min/max) property values for the antenna type to optimize will yield better results.\par
\par
The absolute difference between the actual value and the target value for each property is rescaled to a range from 0 to 10000 and summed according the specified weighting factors. The resulting value is used as input for the optimization process.\par
\par
The optimizer calculates electric-field values in relation to the specified input power value, so optimization is done with a constant power setting and not as might be expected with a constant voltage (or current) setting. If the latter is required, select a power-level of zero watts and specify an appropriate voltage- or current-source.\par
\par
When optimizing for (forward) Gain or F/B-ratio you will have to specify the Theta and Phi angle for which the Gain (and F/B, F/R ratio) should be calculated. If a resolution of 0 degrees is specified the Gain (and F/B ratio) is calculated for this one and only angle. To optimize on F/R-ratio it is required to use a none-zero resolution to enable gain calculation and maximization to be performed for the whole 180 degree backward-region.\par
\par
It is possible to specify a certain forward- and/or backward-region for which the Gain or F/B ratio should be averaged. This is done by specifying a certain resolution in combination with a delta-Phi and/or delta-Theta (d-Phi, d-Theta) value. Averaging is performed for angles between Phi \endash  d_Phi and Phi + d_Phi. The same holds for the Theta and d_Theta angles. The resolution determines how many iteration-steps are made between + d_Theta/Phi and \endash  d_Theta/Phi angles. Using a none-zero resolution will show a considerable increase in required optimization time, because far more gain angles are calculated.\par
\par
\ul Frequency-sweep\par
\ulnone\par
With version 5.1 the possibility to optimize over a certain frequency-band (for instance the 40-meter band) was added. To enable this option, check the \lquote freq-sweep\rquote  box. If done so additional input-boxes are displayed in which you can specify the start- and stop-frequencies and the step-size thus specifying the frequency-band of interest.\par
For each frequency step a complete set of results is calculated. All results for each property are then summed and devided by the freq-step count at the end of the sweep to get the average values to be used as input for the optimization process.\par
\par
\ul Antenna dimensions/characteristics to change\par
\ulnone\par
To optimized one or more of the properties, antenna dimensions/characteristics have to be modified. These dimensions/characteristics are represented by (combinations of) \ldblquote simple\rdblquote  variables. All available \ldblquote simple\rdblquote  variables to optimize are displayed in the upper left box on the settings section. You select one or more variables by clicking on the variable name. Then this variable is added to the box on the right. This box lists all the variables, which are used to optimize the selected antenna properties. You can remove a variable by clicking again on the variable name in the left or right box.\par
\par
When using the genetic optimizer (evolve) you will also have to specify the minimum and maximum allowable variable values. This determines the so called 'search-space', in which feasible solutions are searched. By default the minimim value equals the actual variable value devided by 2, the maximum value equals the actual value times two.\par
\par
\b The traditional optimization process\par
\b0\par
If the traditional optimizing function is selected, clicking the 'Start' button starts the optimizer. First of all some initial calculations are done to determine the sensitive and direction for the selected variables on the requested propertie(s) to optimize. The results of these calculations are shown in the upper right section. After that, the actual optimization is started and the variables are modified with a certain initial step-size to achieve better performance. The results of these optimization steps are displayed in the lower left and right section. \par
\par
The first initial/max step size determines how quickly an optimization solution will be found. A small step size could  lead to a long optimization run. With a too big step size it is possible that with sensitive variables it is possible that the first minimum/maximum is skipped. This value is available in the 'Settings -> optimizer' menu.  Mostly a max/initial step size of 10% will be fine.\par
\par
Optimization steps continue as long as there is still improvement. When there is no further improvement a new sensitive run is performed to determine how the selected variables have to be modified to achieve, if possible, a new better performance and new optimization steps, with smaller step sizes, are done. This continues till the step-size has reached a certain minimum step-size and the optimization process stops. This minimum step size determines how accurate the minimum/maximum will be. This value can be changed in the \lquote\uldb Settings\ulnone\v settings\v0  -> Optimizer' menu. Mostly a value of 0.01% will be fine.\par
\par
By default the delta step size is relative to the active value of the variable to optimize. However it is possible to use absolute (fixed) step sizes. If this option is selected in the Settings -> Optimizer menu, after a sensitivity run, an absolute delta step size is initially calculated and this step size value is not changed until the minimum/maximum is passed and a new sensitivity run is performed.\par
\par
You can examine the optimization results on the two lower output sections on the window. (Intermediate) variable results are stored in the file *.opt on the 4nec2 main home-folder. \par
\par
To save the new optimized variable values to the original *.nec input file, use the 'Update Nec-file' button on the optimizer window. The existing variable values are overwritten with the new values.\par
\par
It is possible to halt the optimizer before the process automatically stops by pressing the 'Halt' button. After the optimizer is stopped or halted you may exit the optimizer or alter, add or remove variable or property settings and continue by pressing the "Resume" button. The optimization process continues with the latest calculated variable values. To (re)start with the initial variable values, you have to exit the optimizer and start it again without saving the results.\par
\tab\par
When only one variable is selected the optimizer can be initially forced in a certain direction to find the next maximum/minimum. This is done by selecting "increase" or "decrease" in the Upper left Function box on the 'Settings" section of the window. After the first maximum/minimum is passed, the normal optimization process is continued.  \par
\par
After optimization it is wise to \uldb validate\ulnone\v IDH_F3\v0  the new geometry structure, and if you updated/saved your results, to perform an additional \uldb Average-Gain-Test\ulnone\v IDH_F7\v0 .\par
\par
\ul Additional Gain and F/B settings\par
\ulnone\par
When selecting Forward gain (Gain), front-to-back (F/B) or front-to-rear ratio (F/R) also the angles which define the forward- or backward-direction and a certain pattern-resolution must be specified. Optionally delta-Theta and delta-Phi values can be specified. This is used to average the forward (and/or backward) gain over a certain region between Theta/Phi - delta_theta/phi and Theta/Phi + delta_theta/phi. The step-size determines the number of averaging steps. (see \uldb Generate\ulnone\v IDH_F7\v0  for additional information. These delta-theta or \endash phi values can be used to minimize both maximum or average side-lobe level in a certain direction.\v )\v0\par
\par
By default when ground is specified, the backward theta/levation angle used for the F/B ratio calculations is set to the same value as the theta angle used for the forward-gain (Theta-back = Theta-forward and Phi back = Phi-forward+180). If free space is selected the backward-angle used is the angle on the opposite side of the XY plane (Theta-back = 180-Theta-forward and Phi-back = Phi-forward+180) It is possible to manually overrule the default backward Theta and/or Phi angles to be used for the F/B ratio calculations.  \par
\par
Furthermore one may include ground wave calculations or select between Horizontal-, Vertical- or total-gain, E-theta, E-Phi or E-radial when performing the Forward gain of front-to-back/rear optimizations.\par
\par
\ul Output log\par
\ulnone\par
During the optimization process, the optimizer variable- and property values (calculated results) are logged in a log file called "Optimizer.log". This log-file is located in the 4nec2 home-folder. This log-file is available for further examination of the results using the \lquote Show\rquote   command button. The next time the optimizer is started, the old log-file is replaced with the new version.\par
\par
To visualize the results and/or behavour of the optimization process one can use the \lquote Plot results\rquote  command-button. After this you will be asked for a property- and one or two variable-names for which a 2D or 3D plot is generated using the \uldb gnuplot\ulnone\v IDH_gpl\v0  program. \par
\par
\par
\ul Tip(s):\par
\ulnone\par
1) During the optimization process you can resize or move the \lquote Optimizer\rquote  window to make more/other data visible. You can 'click' on one of the other windows or use the F2 to F5 key's to bring one or more of the windows in front of the other or you can 'drag' one or more of the windows aside.\par
2) You can only change a certain viewpoint on the 'geometry (F3) or 3D (F9) form or select a specific 2D pattern type on the F4 form, when the optimizer is halted. You can do this before the 'Start' button is used, or you can temporarely pause the optimization after it is started by pressing the 'Halt' button, change setting and press 'Restart'.\par
3) To sequentially switch from Genetic- to Hill-climbing-optimization or back, for the same input-file, use \ldblquote Update Nec-file\rdblquote  to temporarely save the results of the previous run, exit the optimizer and open/load this temporary file as input for the next optimization run. Use \lquote seed with curr-data\rquote  to use existing optimization data in the Genetic optimization process.\par
\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\ri-1800\qj\b\page\super #$K\nosupersub\b0\par
\b\fs24 The variable-sweeper\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\ri-432\qj\b0\fs20\par
In the \uldb optimizer\ulnone\v IDH_optim\v0  window it is possible to evaluate the effect of changing one or more antenna or environmental dimensions. This is done by selecting the \lquote Sweeper\rquote  option in the Function box on the upper left of the Optimizer window. The dimensions to sweep must be specified as \uldb variables\ulnone\v symbol \v0  in the 4nec2 input file.\par
\par
After the Sweeper function is selected, one ore more variables to sweep have to be selected in the \lquote Available variables\rquote  box. For each variable selected it is now possible to specify the min(start) and max(end) values for the sweep. It\rquote s not required for \lquote max\rquote  to be greater than \lquote min\rquote . For instance to sweep from 20 to 10 specify 20 as the \lquote min\rquote -value and 10 as the \lquote max\rquote -value.\par
\par
Furthermore the number of steps for the sweep must be specified. This is done in the \lquote Nr of steps\rquote  Box\rquote . \par
By default the \lquote used fixed stepsize\rquote  box is checked, resulting in equal steps when the variable values are changed from min to max. If un-checked multiplicative steps are used. In this case it is not possible to sweep from <0 to >0 or reverse.\par
\par
The Theta/Elev and Phi/Azim value boxes are used to specify the angles for which the forward gain, F/B-ratio and for none-zero resolutions, the F/R-ratio is calculated. It is furthermore possible to include ground wave data at a specified distance or to select between Horizontal, Vertical and total gain, E-theta, E-Phi or E-radial when sweeping on forward-gain.\par
\par
If a none zero resolution is specified one can select between the horizontal or vertical (or 3D) pattern to be displayed for each step during the sweep. The angle for which the hor/ver pattern is calculated is determined by the specified leftmost Theta/Phi values. All generated patterns can be played-back (using the arrow-keys) after the sweep when the Pattern form (F4) is selected.\par
\par
Because full F/R (and F/B) data is not available when only far-field values are calculated for a certain horizontal or vertical slice, the 3D-pattern option is available. Using this option full gain, F/B and F/R data is generated to be visualized through the \lquote Line-chart (F5)\rquote  window. When this 3D option is selected no horizontal or vertical far-field pattern play-back data is available, because only the last generated full 3D pattern is stored.\par
\par
Sweep output data (to be imported in a spreadsheet or other programs) is also reported through the optimizer/sweeper logfile. To view this data, select \lquote View -> Optimizer log\rquote  on the Geometry window.\par
\par
When a none zero resolution is specified, sweeping speed will decrease because of the additional gain calculations, especially when the 3D option is used.\par
\par
\ul Note:\par
\ulnone\par
Sweeper results are stored in memory only and not on disk (as is the default 4nec2 behavour), so if 4nec2 is shut-down or another (4)nec2 input-files is selected the sweeper results are lost. The log-file is kept till a new optimization or variable-sweep is done.\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\ri-630\qj\f3\page\super #$K\nosupersub\par
\b\i\f0\fs24 Convergence-Test\par
\b0\i0\fs20\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\ri-432\qj During a convergence test the design/model is validated by running it over a number of steps, in which for each next step the number of segments for all wire is increased by a certain amount (20-50%) while Gain, Input-impedance, Efficiency and other values are observed. These values will have to converge towards a certain value.\par
\par
To automatically run such a test select the Conv-Test function on the \uldb Optimizer\ulnone\v IDH_optim\v0  window. If done so, the number of steps to run from default- to the highest allowable number of segments is calculated and displayed in the \lquote Nr of steps\rquote  text-box. When started, on each step the number of segment for wires (for which the highest allowable segmentation is not yet reached) is increased with 33%, till all wires have (almost) reached maximum segmentation. \par
When done, click the \ldblquote Exit\rdblquote  button, and on the \ldblquote Line-chart (F5)\rdblquote  window Gain- and Impedance-data is visualized for further evaluation. Abrupt changes or an increasing slope for one or more of the graphs on the chart could point to an unrealiable model.\par
\par
For a description about how to use the Theta/Elev and  Phi/Azim input boxes (to specify the angles for which Gaind and F/B ratio are calculated) see the \uldb Sweeper\ulnone\v Eval\v0  function.\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\ri-1800\qj\page\super\b #$K\nosupersub\par
\fs24 The Genetic Optimizer\par
\fs20\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\ri-90\qj\b0 The genetic algorithm (GA) used in 4nec2 is a real-value based algorithm. This GA includes a number of selection-, crossover- and mutation-techniques like Roulette-Wheel-, SUS-, or tournament selection, N-point-, Blend- or Simulated binary crossover and  random-, gaussian- or none-uniform mutation.\par
\par
\ul Why use GA\rquote s ?\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\ri-1800\qj\tx9540\ulnone\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\ri-432\qj GA\rquote s are fundamentally different from other optimization techniques. Analytical optimizers usually compute derivatives (hill-climbing/gradient methods) to locate maximum or minimum points for the function being optimized. GA\rquote s  by contrast, create random populations of individuals/chromosomes which are then \lquote evolved\rquote  to converge to an optimum solution. Gradient methods produce one \lquote best\rquote  answer, but they are often trapped in local extremes, which prevent the global optimum from being found. GA\rquote s can produce groups of solutions, no one if which is necessarily the global optimum, but all of which are \lquote good\rquote  solutions.\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\ri-360\qj\par
Furthermore when a large number of  variables is involved with mutual influences among each other, performance of the GA is expected to better than that of the Hill-Climbing optimizer. \par
One drawback of GA\rquote s is that it mostly takes more time to optimize a design using a GA than it takes to optimize using traditional methods.\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\ri-1800\qj\tx9540\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\ri-180\qj\tx9540 Before starting the algorithm, the variables to optimize must be selected, together with their minimum and maximum allowable values (limits). These user-selectable upper- and lower- limits define the boundaries for the \lquote search-space\rquote . Mostly these limits are set according physical limits, like available space, maximum height or width etc.\par
However, setting a too wide range may slow down the GA and might cause it to miss a good solution, because it searched not long enough. Setting a too narrow range might exclude useful and interesting solutions. Experience in this is the most important factor and insight in what may affect antenna performance is also very usefull.\par
\par
Each variable to optimize is called a gene. All the genes together form the chromosome. When the GA is started an initial random population is created by uniform random selection of the gene value on the interval between the lower and upper variable limits.\par
\par
A population is a group of N chromosomes or individuals. Each individual representing a different 4nec2 antenna design. The GA \lquote evolves\rquote  the initial random population by creating successive generations of individuals. The population size N is constant from generation to generation, but the individuals comprising the population are genetically altered from one generation to the other.\par
\par
One of the most important features of a GA is its ability to optimize a population using any desired measure of \lquote goodness\rquote . Unlike gradient methods GA\rquote s easily handle discontinuous functions. The only requirement for a useful \lquote Figure Of  Merit\rquote  (FOM) is that it is calculable. The data used for the FOM is usually generated by a separate computer program (calculation engine) that models the system being optimized and not by the GA itself. The GA inputs data from the engine and uses this to computer the FOM or fitness values. This procedure is also known as the \lquote objective\rquote  function.\par
\par
The FOM calculation used with the GA is the same as the one used with the traditional hill-climbing 4nec2 optimizer.\par
For one or more antenna properties a weighting factor from 0 to 100 (or more) percent is specified by the user, together with an optional target value (click on property using right mouse-button). The absolute difference between the actual value and the target value for each property is then rescaled to a range from 0 to 10000 and summed according the specified weighting factors. The resulting value is then used as input for the rank-based fitness calculations as described below.\par
\par
Generation after generation is created this way with the expectation that each successive generation will contain better antenna designs. But because of its stochastic nature a GA may not create improved designs in every successive generation, and the FOM/fitness-value may actually go down. Experience has demonstrated that a well-designed GA will converge to an optimum solution if allowed to run for enough generations with a sufficient large population size.\ul\b\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\qj\ulnone\b0\par
\pard\keepn\nowidctlpar\s3\qj\b GA parameters\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\qj\b0\par
All parameters used by the 4nec2 GA are available on the \lquote Optimizer\rquote  form (F12). It may be required to click the \lquote GA\rquote  (option) button to make them visible.\par
\b\par
\ul\b0 Max-generation.\par
\ulnone\par
This parameter sets the maximum number of generation after which the evolution of new populations will stop. You are now able to save any of the resultant antenna design files or exit the optimizer and/or consult the summary log-file for all population data (use 'Show Log' button). You can also resume the generation of new populations by pressing the 'Resume' button. A new set of \lquote max-generation\rquote  populations is now processed.  \par
\par
You may halt Evolution, before the max number of generations is reached. For instance because the solutions have converged, an adequate solution is available or if you want to change one or more of the Genetic Operators and resume. \par
\b\par
\ul\b0 Halt if N % same fitness\par
\par
\ulnone During the GA evolution process, the overall fitness of all the individuals will increase and finally converge around a certain solution. At this point most of the chromosomes (with exception of the onces just mutated) will have a similar fitness. You can specify to halt the optimization if a certain percentage of individuals have reached the same maximum fitness value. Mostly this value will be somewhere around 50-90%. To be sure all generations as specified by the \lquote max-generation\rquote  setting are evolved, set this percentage to 100%. \par
\ul\par
Population size\par
\ulnone\par
This parameter specifies the number of chromosomes (individual antenna designs) included in each population. As a general rule, values in the range 30-200 provide a good starting point, depending on the number of segments and how fast a CPU is available.\par
\par
\ul Seed with current data\par
\ulnone\f3\par
\f0 This option is used to seed the coming GA run, thereby initiating the GA search around a particular solution in its search space. The \lquote seed\rquote  individual (design) is inserted as individual #1 in generation #1. Its function is similar to the \lquote Elitism\rquote  function as described below.\par
\par
\ul Save best file for last N generations\par
\par
\ulnone When a GA run is completed either because it was aborted or because the maximum number of generations was reached, all individual *.nec antenna designs for the last population are available in the \ldblquote\\opt\rdblquote  sub-folder below your current *.nec input-file folder. Furthermore for a specified number of last generations the *.nec file for the best individual is saved. This number of last generation is specified in the field \ldblquote save best file for last .N. generations\rdblquote .\par
\par
\ul Update Nec-file button\par
\ulnone\par
This command button is used to let the user select the best file from last N generations. After this the original *.nec input file is updated with the new variable-values from the selected file and the results are saved to a file with the same or a different name. This file could then be used as a temporary or final result or as a seed for subsequent GA runs.\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\ri-1800\qj\tx9540\ul\b\par
\ulnone GA specific parameters\par
\ul\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\ri-90\qj\tx9540\ulnone\b0 The other GA specific parameters are described in the more general \uldb Genetic Algorithms\ulnone\v GA\v0  section. These parameters are supplied for those who want to further explore the (dis)advantages of Genetic Algorithms for optimization of antenna designs. \par
\par
It is strongly recommended that every one using the GA optimizer reads this section.\par
\par
As a good starting point one could use the below settings:\par
\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\qj\tab - Linear Ranking\par
\tab - Roulette Wheel Selection, chromosomes to re-use=1\par
\tab - Blend Crossover, alpha = 0.5, probability = 70%\par
\tab - Uniform mutation, probability = 4%\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\ri-90\qj\tx9540\par
With all possible ranking-, selection-, crossover- or mutation-operators you will be able to get good results. However specification of very different crossover- or mutation probabilities may result in unsuccesfull or unwanted evolution of your design. \par
\pard\nowidctlpar\ri-1800\qj\tx9540\ul\b\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\ri-1800\ulnone\b0\page\super #$K\nosupersub\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\ri-1800\qj\b\fs24 Capture and Save or Print form/window (picture viewer)\par
\b0\fs20\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\ri-432\qj With version 5.4.1 or later you can capture any form, window or other part of the screen for printing or saving as a bitmap file. You can also copy the captured part of the screen to the clipboard to paste it into your favorite text- or graphic processing tool. As an extra feature you can also paste any other image placed on the clipboard back into the 4nec2 capturing tool for printing or saving.\par
\par
With version 5.6, additional functionallity (picture viewer) was added to capture a range of pictures/images. Each additional picture pasted from the clipboard is added to the already existing range of pictures/images. If two or more pictures are pasted, a scroll-bar is visualized with which you can scroll through the range of pictures. You can also use the right- and left-arrow key for this. The \lquote 0\rquote  to \lquote 9\rquote  number-keys are available for direct switching between two separate pictures. This picture-viewer function is used in conjunction with the ItsHF propagation prediction software to \lquote animate\rquote  the propagation-changes during the day, the year, between different frequencies or as the result of a antenna-variable sweep.\par
 \par
To capture and print/save the current/active 4nec2 form, use the \ldblquote Show -> Print\rdblquote  menu-bar option on each form or use the Ctrl+P key\rquote s. A new pop-up window in displayed in which, by default, as a small image the captured form is visible. Then, use the \ldblquote Print\rdblquote  button to print the image on your default printer or use \ldblquote Save\rdblquote  to save the image as a bitmap file. \par
\par
The following function/buttons are available\par
\par
- Form\tab\tab To capture the last active 4nec2 form/window. (first select window, then use button)\par
- Window\tab To capture any other (none 4nec2) window. (click button first, then select window)\par
- Screen\tab\tab To capture the whole screen.\par
\par
- Clear\tab\tab To clear the captured image\par
- Sel.\tab\tab To switch to full-size image and select partial image to print/save\par
\par
- Print\tab\tab To print the captured image on your default printer\par
- Save\tab\tab To save the captured image as a bitmap (*.bmp) file\par
\par
- Copy\tab\tab Copy the image to the clipboard.\par
- Paste\tab\tab Paste an image from the clipboard for printing or saving.\par
\par
When \ldblquote Window\rdblquote  is selected you will have to activate/select the window you would like to capture for printing or saving. After the message-box is closed you will have to seconds to do so.\par
\par
When you captured a complete form/window or screen and would like to print/save only part of this image, use the \lquote Sel\rquote (ect) button. The image will change to full-size and while pushing the left-mouse button you can select any part of the image for printing or saving. Use \lquote Res\rquote (et) to reset the selection and/or try again.\par
\par
When using Hewlett-Packard (HP) LaserJet Series II printers it is possible that a general protection fault is generated or that only a blank page is printed. To eliminate this problem, reset the color depth of the video driver on your System to 256 colors or less, or 8 bits per pixel or less. You can do this by changing the display properties (Click left mouse button on blank part of your desktop)\par
\par
You can also use the \uldb gnuplot\ulnone\v IDH_gpl\v0  program to directly print 2D and/or 3D graphs/plots through the \lquote plot\rquote  menu-option/command.  \par
\pard\nowidctlpar\ri-1800\qj\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\qj\up10\fs13\page\super\up0\fs20 #$K\nosupersub\f3\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\ri-1800\qj\b\f0\fs24 gnuplot\tab\tab The gnuplot plotting program\par
\b0\fs20\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\ri-432\qj In version 5.4.0 and later the use of a separate freeware plotting program called \lquote gnuplot\rquote  was added to the 4nec2 package. The gnuplot program is used by 4nec2 to plot and print 2D and 3D charts/graphs for various data available through the \ldblquote plot\rdblquote  menu-bar option on the \ldblquote Geometry (F3)\rdblquote , \ldblquote Pattern (F4)\rdblquote , \ldblquote Line- chart (F5)\rdblquote  windows and through the \ldblquote plot\rdblquote  button on the \ldblquote Optimizer (F12)\rdblquote  window.\par
\par
However, before 4nec2 is able to use the gnuplot program, it has the be downloaded from the Internet at \cf2\ul http://www.gnuplot.info/download.html\cf0\ulnone  and installed on your system.  After this the gnuplot main directory/folder path has to be specified in 4nec2 by selecting \ldblquote Setting -> Folders -> gnuplot\rdblquote  on the \ldblquote Main (F2)\rdblquote  window. See \uldb Settings\ulnone\v settings\v0  for additional info.\par
\par
When a plot request is made for a certain data-item two additional windows are displayed. The \ldblquote plotting options\rdblquote  window in which a number of plot-options can be changed and the \ldblquote gnuplot graph\rdblquote  window itself in which the resulting graph(s) is/are displayed. \par
\par
In the \ldblquote gnuplot graph\rdblquote  window (which belongs to the gnuplot program), when a 2D graph is displayed you can use the right mouse-button to zoom-in on the graph. Use the \ldblquote u\rdblquote  key to unzoom and/or reset. When a 3D graph is displayed you can use the left mouse-button to rotate the graph. \par
\par
To \b print\b0  the current plot/graph click on the graph icon located on the left at the title-bar for the \ldblquote gnuplot graph\rdblquote  window and select \lquote Options->Print\rdblquote\par
\par
In the \ldblquote plotting options\rdblquote  window you can set/switch the following plotting options:\par
\par
\b General options:\par
\b0\par
- Color\tab\tab\tab To switch plotting color(s)\par
- Line-width\tab\tab To change the width of the plotted lines and/or points\par
- Style\tab\tab\tab To switch between different plotting styles\par
- Point-type\tab\tab To switch point-types for \lquote points\rquote  and \lquote linespoints; plotting-style\par
- Smooth\tab\tab To \lquote smooth\rquote  the graph by selecting a smoothing algorithm.\par
- Grid-lines\tab\tab To enable/disable the grid-lines display.\par
\par
- Open plot\tab\tab To open/load a previously saved plot/graph.\par
- Add plot\tab\tab To add a previously saved plot/graph to the current plot.\par
- Save plot\tab\tab To save the current plot/graph for future use.\par
- Rem plot\tab\tab To remove/delete the last added plot/graph from the current plot.\par
\par
\b 2D options\par
\b0\par
- Log X-axis\tab\tab To switch between a linear or logarithmic X-axis \par
- Log Y-axis\tab\tab To switch between a linear or logarithmic Y-axis \par
- Double Y-axis\tab single(left) or double(left+right) Y-axis when two or more plots loaded\par
- Y2 or Y1 grid\tab To switch between left(Y1)- or right(Y2) Y-axis grid-lines.\par
\b\par
3D options\par
\b0\par
- View as map\tab To switch between a 3D or a 2D map-view.\par
- Size\tab\tab\tab To change the size of the 3D or map-view.\par
- Show surface\tab To switch between grid- or a closed-surface 3D style.\par
- Log Z-axis\tab\tab To switch between a linear or logarithmic vertical Z-axis \par
- Contour plot\tab To display contour lines on the 3D or map-view\par
- Nr cont\tab\tab To change the number of contour lines to display.\par
- Surface colors\tab To switch between color mapping styles.\par
- Color style\tab\tab To select between different color maps.\par
- Make surface\tab To create full 3D surface when only limited (scattered) 3D data is available\par
\par
All plotting commands generated by 4nec2 are send to gnuplot through a third (normally minimized) command window \ldblquote gnuplot\rdblquote . You can open/restore this window in case of problems and/or if you are curious about the commands used. But before doing so, I would suggest to first consult the gnuplot help-files available in the \lquote ..\\gnuplot\\doc\rquote  folder.\par
\par
For those interested in the effectiveness of the optimization algorithms used by 4nec2 it is possible to use the \lquote plot\rquote  button after an optimization run (hill-climb or genetic) is done and visualize the search results or 3D search-space\'85.\par
\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\qj\page\super #$K\nosupersub\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\ri-1800\qj\b\fs24 POVRAY\tab Generate true 3D views\par
\fs20\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\ri-432\qj\b0 With this feature you are able to generate true 3D views for your geometry structure or the far field radiation pattern.\par
\pard This feature works in conjunction with the Povray ray-tracing software, freely available on the internet at \f3{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.povray.com "}}{\fldrslt{\cf2\ul\f0\fs24 http://www.povray.com/}}}\cf0\ulnone\f3\fs20\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\ri-1800\qj\f0\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\ri-432\qj The povray files are generated under the \ldblquote ..\\4nec2\\pov\rdblquote  folder. These files are based on the default povray file named default.pov under the \ldblquote ..\\4nec2\\exe\rdblquote  folder. In this default file the settings for the light source and the camera position are described. For the more experienced povray users it is possible to modify this default file to get other light or camera settings.\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\ri-1800\qj\par
